{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/main-numbers/reader-packet.json\\\")); print(json.dumps(p,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))'\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"articles\": [\n    {\n      \"article\": 1,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n      \"title\": \"AMD Stock Has Blown Past Nvidia This Year. Is It Still the Better Buy?\",\n      \"text\": \"Advanced Micro Devices (NASDAQ:AMD) recently joined the trillion-dollar club, as the stock has been on an absolute tear this year, rising by nearly 190% thus far in 2026. By comparison, longtime rival Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA), which has a market cap of around $5.5 trillion, has risen at a more modest rate of 23%.\\nAMD is a fraction of the size of Nvidia, but it also isn't nearly as big in terms of the revenue or profit it generates. Is it still the better buy moving forward, or are investors better off going with Nvidia instead?\\nThe market is bullish about AMD's newest products\\nBoth AMD and Nvidia sell artificial intelligence (AI) chips that are in high demand. But for a while, the market appeared hesitant about AMD. In 2024, while Nvidia rose by more than 170%, AMD's stock actually declined by 18%. It was toward the latter part of last year when AMD's stock began to outperform Nvidia, and that trend has extended into this year.\\nThe launch of the company's new Helios rack-scale AI system appears to have investors bullish about AMD's ability to compete with Nvidia. AMD launched the AI infrastructure solution a few months ago, and expectations are high that it will generate strong growth. AMD is already coming off a strong quarter for the period ending June 27, with revenue of $11.5 billion, up 50% year over year. However, for the current quarter, it's projecting a slightly slower growth rate of around 41%.\\nThe hype around AMD has sent the tech stock to a new all-time high this week. If Helios can continue delivering strong growth numbers and ends up being the catalyst many investors hope for, it may not be inconceivable to expect AMD to continue outperforming its larger rival. There is one thing that may get in the way, however: valuation.\\nNvidia's stock is far cheaper, based on earnings\\nAMD's stock is running hot, but it's also running more on hype than what its financials might justify. Even based on analyst projections of future profits, it's trading at a forward price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple of around 40. By comparison, Nvidia's forward P/E is only 25, and the S&P 500 average is 20. Investors are paying a much higher premium for AMD.\\nMeanwhile, even if its growth rate accelerates, it might not catch Nvidia, anyway -- its sales more than doubled in its most recent quarter. Not only is Nvidia already much larger than AMD, but it's also growing at a significantly faster rate.\\nInvestors may be focusing too much on market cap, believing that AMD is a cheap buy, rather than earnings multiples, which tell a much different story. AMD may be lighter in valuation, but so too are its profits; in the trailing 12 months, it has amassed $6.4 billion in net income while Nvidia has generated $192.9 billion. These companies just aren't even in the same ballpark.\\nOnce investors dig into the financials of these companies, it becomes abundantly clear that one stock is a far better deal than the other -- Nvidia -- and it isn't even close. Nvidia is already a leader in the AI chip market, and AMD is simply trying to prove it can keep up and offer formidable competition. That certainly doesn't warrant the stock trading at such a higher valuation than Nvidia (with respect to earnings).\\nAMD's stock may be rallying this year, but I don't think it will continue to outperform Nvidia in the long run. It's trading at an inflated valuation, and with high expectations for Helios, there's significant downside risk in the stock. Nvidia is a much safer option for growth investors and is likely to produce better returns from here on out.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 2,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n      \"title\": \"NVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) Is a Trending Stock: Facts to Know Before Betting on It\",\n      \"text\": \"Shares of this maker of graphics chips for gaming and artificial intelligence have returned +9.1% over the past month versus the Zacks S&P 500 composite's +1.3% change. The Zacks Semiconductor - General industry, to which Nvidia belongs, has gained 8% over this period. Now the key question is: Where could the stock be headed in the near term?\\nAlthough media reports or rumors about a significant change in a company's business prospects usually cause its stock to trend and lead to an immediate price change, there are always certain fundamental factors that ultimately drive the buy-and-hold decision.\\nRather than focusing on anything else, we at Zacks prioritize evaluating the change in a company's earnings projection. This is because we believe the fair value for its stock is determined by the present value of its future stream of earnings.\\nOur analysis is essentially based on how sell-side analysts covering the stock are revising their earnings estimates to take the latest business trends into account. When earnings estimates for a company go up, the fair value for its stock goes up as well. And when a stock's fair value is higher than its current market price, investors tend to buy the stock, resulting in its price moving upward. Because of this, empirical studies indicate a strong correlation between trends in earnings estimate revisions and short-term stock price movements.\\nFor the current quarter, Nvidia is expected to post earnings of $2.47 per share, indicating a change of +90% from the year-ago quarter. The Zacks Consensus Estimate has changed +5.8% over the last 30 days.\\nFor the current fiscal year, the consensus earnings estimate of $9.25 points to a change of +93.9% from the prior year. Over the last 30 days, this estimate has changed +5.1%.\\nFor the next fiscal year, the consensus earnings estimate of $15.33 indicates a change of +65.8% from what Nvidia is expected to report a year ago. Over the past month, the estimate has changed +21.8%.\\nWhile earnings growth is arguably the most superior indicator of a company's financial health, nothing happens as such if a business isn't able to grow its revenues. After all, it's nearly impossible for a company to increase its earnings for an extended period without increasing its revenues. So, it's important to know a company's potential revenue growth.\\nFor Nvidia, the consensus sales estimate for the current quarter of $109.02 billion indicates a year-over-year change of +91.2%. For the current and next fiscal years, $406.05 billion and $671.95 billion estimates indicate +88% and +65.5% changes, respectively.\\nNo investment decision can be efficient without considering a stock's valuation. Whether a stock's current price rightly reflects the intrinsic value of the underlying business and the company's growth prospects is an essential determinant of its future price performance.\\nWhile comparing the current values of a company's valuation multiples, such as price-to-earnings (P/E), price-to-sales (P/S), and price-to-cash flow (P/CF), with its own historical values helps determine whether its stock is fairly valued, overvalued, or undervalued, comparing the company relative to its peers on these parameters gives a good sense of the reasonability of the stock's price.\\nThe Zacks Value Style Score (part of the Zacks Style Scores system), which pays close attention to both traditional and unconventional valuation metrics to grade stocks from A to F (an A is better than a B; a B is better than a C; and so on), is pretty helpful in identifying whether a stock is overvalued, rightly valued, or temporarily undervalued.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": [\n        {\n          \"fact\": 1,\n          \"text\": \"the consensus sales estimate of $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"fact\": 2,\n          \"text\": \"the consensus sales estimate of $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 3,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n      \"title\": \"Nvidia Gains as Apple Pulls AI Off the Meter\",\n      \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI-accelerator and data-center platform leader, faced a fresh edge-computing test as Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) pushed its new Macs as machines capable of running heavyweight AI privately instead of constantly calling the cloud. Nvidia shares gained approximately 0.7% to $229.08, suggesting investors were hardly treating Apple's move as an immediate threat to the GPU giant's core data-center engine.\\nWarning! GuruFocus has detected 3 Warning Sign with MSFT.\\nApple showed four Mac Studios working together to run a trillion-parameter model, using shared memory across the machines to tackle workloads once associated almost entirely with expensive server infrastructure. That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI. The GuruFocus picture reinforces that underlying strength, with Nvidia carrying a GF Score of 95/100 and particularly strong readings for profitability, growth and financial strength, while GF Value remains the noticeably weaker part of the profile.\\nThe bigger question is not whether a handful of Macs can run a giant model. They clearly can. The question is how much inference, coding and repetitive enterprise AI work permanently shifts from cloud infrastructure onto local machines. Apple could chip away at some private and predictable workloads, especially where data security and recurring token costs matter. But hyperscale training, massive networking demands and unpredictable peak workloads still play directly into Nvidia's territory. For Nvidia investors, the real risk starts only if local AI becomes a structural substitute for cloud inference rather than another layer of the computing stack.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 4,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n      \"title\": \"Top Analyst Reports for NVIDIA, Berkshire Hathaway & GE Aerospace\",\n      \"text\": \"Tuesday, September 22, 2026\\nThe Zacks Research Daily presents the best research output of our analyst team. Today's Research Daily features new research reports on 16 major stocks, including NVIDIA Corp. (NVDA), Berkshire Hathaway Inc. (BRK.B) and GE Aerospace (GE). These research reports have been hand-picked from the roughly 70 reports published by our analyst team today.\\nYou can see all of today's research reports here >>>\\nThe daily 'Ahead of Wall Street' article is a must-read for all investors who would like to be ready for that day's trading action. The article comes out before the market opens, attempting to make sense of that morning's economic releases and how they will affect that day's market action. You can read this article for free on our home page and can actually sign up there to get an email notification as this article comes out each morning.\\nYou can read today's AWS here >>> Still Waters Bring Mixed Pre-Market Levels\\nShares of NVIDIA have gained +27.8% over the past year against the Zacks Semiconductor - General industry's gain of +35.6%. The company continues to benefit from broad demand for accelerated computing as customers build AI factories across hyperscalers, AI clouds, enterprises and sovereign buyers.\\nBlackwell Ultra is supporting growth while Vera Rubin broadens the platform across GPUs, CPUs, networking and software. Customer diversification, rising system content and usage-linked opportunities extend the runway, while cash generation supports capital returns. China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\nCustomer concentration, rising operating investment and competition add uncertainty. Even so, platform leadership, product cadence and expanding AI infrastructure demand give the positive drivers greater weight in the current risk-reward profile.\\n(You can read the full research report on NVIDIA here >>> )\\nBerkshire Hathaway's shares have gained +1.6% over the past year against the Zacks Insurance - Property and Casualty industry's gain of +2.8%. The company benefits from diversified businesses, disciplined underwriting and a large liquidity base that support durable earnings capacity and flexible capital allocation. Insurance float remains a low-cost funding source, while manufacturing, services, BNSF and Berkshire Hathaway Energy broaden the earnings mix.\\nRecent acquisitions and renewed share repurchases show multiple avenues for deploying capital. Still, insurance results remain exposed to catastrophe losses and rising claim frequency and severity. Lower short-term rates are reducing insurance investment income, while BNSF and BHE require substantial capital spending.\\nGreg Abel is now leading the company, leaving execution under the new leadership structure as an important watchpoint. These offsets support an overall balanced risk-reward profile and a Neutral view.\\n(You can read the full research report on Berkshire Hathaway here >>> )\\nShares of GE Aerospace have outperformed the Zacks Aerospace - Defense industry over the past year (+4.9% vs. -13.2%). The company has been witnessing strength in its businesses, driven by robust demand for commercial engines, propulsion and additive technologies. Rising U.S. & international defense budgets, geopolitical tensions, positive airline & airframer dynamics and robust demand for commercial air travel augur well for the company.\\nGE Aerospace's portfolio-reshaping actions are likely to unlock value for its shareholders. The company raised its dividend by 30.6% to 36 cents per share in February 2026.\\nHowever, it has been dealing with high costs and expenses related to certain projects and restructuring activities, which are likely to affect its margins and profitability. Supply-chain disruptions may also result in delays and increased costs. Given GE Aerospace's international presence, foreign exchange headwinds might be worrying for the company.\\n(You can read the full research report on GE Aerospace here >>> )\\nOther noteworthy reports we are featuring today include Intel Corp. (INTC), Morgan Stanley (MS) and Arm Holdings plc (ARM).\\nPer the Zacks analyst, strong demand for AI and data-center infrastructure is likely to support Intel's top line. Its focus on AI-ready computing and advanced networking solutions is positive.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, rising adoption of compute platforms across cloud artificial intelligence is benefiting ARM. However, Elevated expenses due to increased investments remain a concern.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Dollar Tree is benefiting from broad category growth, multi-price adoption and store improvements, while measured expansion and reinvestment support future sales.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Textron gains from diversified exposure across business aviation, helicopters, defense platforms and aftermarket services, supported by strong demand and defense modernization.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Antero Resources' expansion of its core West Virginia Marcellus position enhances its production outlook. However, its exposure to commodity price volatility raises concerns.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Hexcel Corporation is likely to benefit from the improving air traffic. Yet labor shortage result in delays and likely impact operating results.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, SL Green benefits from strong leasing, rising rents and broad tenant demand. However, elevated debt, interest expenses and refinancing exposure remain material risks.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, solid IB business, strategic alliances/acquisitions, global footprint and Morgan Stanley's focus on less capital-markets-dependent operations will likely support its financials.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, growing 800G demand and 1.6T FiberEdge and CopperEdge ramps are driving Semtech's data center growth.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, strong Xifaxan growth, strategic acquisitions and new product approvals fuel Bausch Health's growth prospects. However, uncertainty around Bausch + Lomb separation is a concern.\\nThe Zacks analyst is concerned with Boston Scientific facing a sharp U.S. WATCHMAN market slowdown, as higher clinical evidence impacts referral trends. Portfolio gaps within CRM are also worrying.\\nPer the Zacks Analyst, Rigetti faces rising losses, execution and dilution risks, though cloud expansion, Novera sales, government ties and a solid cash position support longer-term potential.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, NVR faces housing affordability constraints, softer settlements and margin pressure. Also, lower home prices and higher lot costs remain concerns.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 5,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n      \"title\": \"Nvidia stock is approaching its cheapest valuation in a decade despite booming profits\",\n      \"text\": \"Nvidia stock is trading at less than 17 times forward earnings, half the multiple it commanded in 2025, even as revenue and net income are expected to surge\\nNvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\\nAt under 17 times forward earnings, the stock's multiple has been cut in half compared with 2025 — a period when Nvidia 's expansion was actually slower — and has fallen sharply from above 25 times expected profits just this past May, according to Bloomberg.\\n\\\"The stock has de-rated pretty significantly, which suggests a healthy dose of skepticism that the company's current earnings power is sustainable,\\\" Eli Horton, a TCW senior portfolio manager overseeing thematic and durable growth equities, told Bloomberg. \\\"The stock's performance is surprising, given the backdrop of incredible fundamentals, but it tells you the market is expecting less than what the consensus is currently estimating.\\\"\\nOne drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\\nCompetition is another concern. David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, pointed to the growing threat of customers building their own silicon, noting that Meta $META Platforms and Alphabet $GOOGL are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally — a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia 's pricing power and market share. \\\"Multiples expand when companies are well positioned with potential to get better, and Nvidia doesn't offer that,\\\" Russell said.\\nNvidia stock has gained 22% so far in 2026, putting it second among the Magnificent Seven behind Apple $AAPL 's 25% advance — yet that performance looks modest against the broader chip sector. The Philadelphia Stock Exchange Semiconductor Index has surged nearly 76%, with Intel $INTC and Advanced Micro Devices both more than tripling; Nvidia sits near the bottom of the index's leaderboard, even as the index itself carries a valuation of 20 times forward earnings.\\nNvidia Chief Executive Officer Jensen Huang has pushed back on the market's assessment, calling the company \\\"the world's first and only growth value stock\\\" and describing Nvidia as \\\"incredibly misunderstood\\\" at a Goldman Sachs $GS technology conference earlier this month.\\nThe valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\\nHorton said the current multiple looks favorable given that neither a pullback in AI infrastructure spending nor a major regulatory intervention appears imminent. \\\"This seems like a very favorable multiple to have as an entry point,\\\" he said.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": [\n        {\n          \"fact\": 1,\n          \"text\": \"the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, against the 45% analysts had expected\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 6,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n      \"title\": \"Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n      \"text\": \"Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n          \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 7,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n      \"title\": \"Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24\",\n      \"text\": \"Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" three suggest a \\\"Moderate Buy,\\\" and one offers a \\\"Strong Sell\\\" rating.\\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n          \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n          \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 8,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n      \"title\": \"NVIDIA's $279B Supply Commitments: Can These Secure Its Growth Runway?\",\n      \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corporation 's NVDA increase in supply and capacity commitments signals confidence in sustained artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure demand. At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. This gives NVIDIA greater visibility into component availability as it ramps up Blackwell and the next-generation Vera Rubin platform.\\nThe timing is important. NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion. The company expects third-quarter revenues of $108 billion (+/- 2%), indicating 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\\nThe supply commitments could help NVIDIA avoid shortages that might otherwise limit revenue growth. Securing memory and critical components in advance should support smoother production as AI customers expand data center deployments. NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\\nHowever, the commitments create risks. NVIDIA could face higher costs or excess inventory if customer demand slows or product transitions occur faster than expected. The company warns that changing demand could make commitments difficult to reduce.\\nOverall, the $279 billion commitment provides NVIDIA with a stronger supply base for its AI growth plans, but execution and demand will determine how effectively that capacity converts into revenues. The Zacks Consensus Estimate for fiscal 2027 revenues is currently pegged at $406.05 billion, indicating an 88% year-over-year increase.\\nAMD and Intel Are Challenging NVIDIA in AI Race\\nAdvanced Micro Devices, Inc. AMD and Intel Corporation INTC are expanding their AI businesses.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices is broadening its data center AI opportunity from Instinct accelerators to the Helios rack-scale platform. In the second quarter of 2026, AMD's Data Center revenues rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion, while Instinct sales more than doubled. Helios is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027.\\nThe Helios platform has already garnered multiple large deals from big tech companies. Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of MI450-series GPUs using the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. Microsoft plans to deploy Helios at scale on Azure. Advanced Micro Devices expects Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027.\\nIntel is also gaining momentum in data center AI. Its second-quarter revenues rose 25% to $16.13 billion, while Data Center and AI revenues surged 59% to $6.26 billion. Intel also launched Xeon 6+ and expanded its open-source OpenVINO Physical AI framework, giving developers tools for AI and robotics applications.\\nIntel is building a broader AI infrastructure platform spanning CPUs, ASICs, graphics, networking and advanced packaging as customers design workload-specific systems. In the second quarter of 2026, purpose-built silicon revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year. During the last earnings call, management noted that purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate, and the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future.\\nShares of NVIDIA have risen around 20.8% year to date, underperforming the Zacks Computer and Technology sector's gain of 23.4%.\\nFrom a valuation standpoint, NVDA trades at a forward price-to-earnings ratio of 17.11, below the sector's average of 21.45.\\nThe Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 earnings implies a year-over-year increase of approximately 93.9% and 65.8%, respectively. Estimates for fiscal 2027 and 2028 have been revised upward over the past 30 days.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n          \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n          \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n          \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": [\n        {\n          \"fact\": 1,\n          \"text\": \"the Zacks consensus of $406.05 billion for fiscal 2027 revenues\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"fact\": 2,\n          \"text\": \"the company expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 9,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n      \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\",\n      \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n          \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n          \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n          \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": [\n        {\n          \"fact\": 1,\n          \"text\": \"management guided about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"fact\": 2,\n          \"text\": \"the guide has the margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"article\": 10,\n      \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n      \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n      \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\",\n      \"questions_already_open\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n          \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n          \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n          \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n          \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n          \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"named_facts\": [\n        {\n          \"fact\": 1,\n          \"text\": \"analysts' consensus of about $412 billion for fiscal 2027 revenue\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"fact\": 2,\n          \"text\": \"the CFO expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"fact\": 3,\n          \"text\": \"gross margins to settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A01\",\n      \"article\": 4,\n      \"new_questions\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-supply-commitments\",\n          \"question\": \"What total of supply commitments and purchase obligations will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of supply commitments/purchase obligations NVIDIA has taken on, as reported in its next annual report, and how it compares with prior disclosures.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next annual report (Form 10-K), which discloses purchase obligations and supply commitments.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A02\",\n      \"article\": 10,\n      \"new_questions\": [],\n      \"kept_statements\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"e1\",\n          \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of fiscal Q1 — with the data center business accounting for $89 billion of the total, and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as reported in the company's quarterly results.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"clean-5\",\n              \"new\": false,\n              \"why\": \"NVIDIA's data center revenue and its 106% growth are an output of AI infrastructure spending by hyperscalers and others; they corroborate that calendar-2026 AI data center capex is running strong, but they do not measure the five hyperscalers' combined capex tracked by the thesis.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"e2\",\n          \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, which the article states is around 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": []\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"e3\",\n          \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n              \"new\": false,\n              \"why\": \"Management states that growth is limited by manufacturing capacity, not demand, which bears on whether NVIDIA takes on larger purchase and supply commitments to secure capacity; the statement gives no amount and does not quantify the commitments the thesis tracks.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"e4\",\n          \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said on the company's late-August earnings call that gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect, down from 75% in the fiscal second quarter.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": []\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"e5\",\n          \"summary\": \"The article states that analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, and applying the guided roughly 70% growth to that figure derives fiscal 2028 revenue of somewhere around $700 billion.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": []\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A03\",\n      \"article\": 2,\n      \"new_questions\": [],\n      \"kept_statements\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev1\",\n          \"summary\": \"Zacks reported that the consensus expects NVIDIA to post earnings of $2.47 per share in the current quarter, a +90% change from the year-ago quarter, and that the Zacks Consensus Estimate has risen +5.8% over the last 30 days.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"For the current quarter, Nvidia is expected to post earnings of $2.47 per share, indicating a change of +90% from the year-ago quarter. The Zacks Consensus Estimate has changed +5.8% over the last 30 days.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": []\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev2\",\n          \"summary\": \"Zacks reported that the consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's current fiscal year is $9.25 per share, implying a +93.9% change from the prior year, and that this estimate has risen +5.1% over the last 30 days.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"For the current fiscal year, the consensus earnings estimate of $9.25 points to a change of +93.9% from the prior year. Over the last 30 days, this estimate has changed +5.1%.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": []\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev3\",\n          \"summary\": \"Zacks reported that the consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's next fiscal year is $15.33 per share, a +65.8% change from what NVIDIA is expected to report a year earlier, and that this estimate has risen +21.8% over the past month.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"For the next fiscal year, the consensus earnings estimate of $15.33 indicates a change of +65.8% from what Nvidia is expected to report a year ago. Over the past month, the estimate has changed +21.8%.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": []\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev4\",\n          \"summary\": \"Zacks reported that the consensus sales estimate for NVIDIA's current quarter is $109.02 billion, indicating a +91.2% year-over-year change.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"For Nvidia, the consensus sales estimate for the current quarter of $109.02 billion indicates a year-over-year change of +91.2%.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": []\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev5\",\n          \"summary\": \"Zacks reported that consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA are $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year, indicating changes of +88% and +65.5%, respectively.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"For the current and next fiscal years, $406.05 billion and $671.95 billion estimates indicate +88% and +65.5% changes, respectively.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": []\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A04\",\n      \"article\": 10,\n      \"new_questions\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-fy2028-revenue\",\n          \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028) revenue grow roughly 70% year over year from fiscal 2027, as CFO Colette Kress guided on the late-August 2026 earnings call, or will the supply-constrained outlook produce a different total?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 total revenue and its growth rate versus fiscal 2027, compared with the roughly 70% growth the CFO guided (implying roughly $700 billion if the ~$412 billion fiscal 2027 consensus holds).\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal year 2028 (ending January 2028); settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter earnings release and Form 10-K (annual report), expected around February 2028.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-fy2028-gross-margin\",\n          \"question\": \"Where will NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 gross margin settle, versus the 72%–73% CFO Colette Kress guided and the 75% reported in fiscal 2027's second quarter?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin and the fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter trough, compared with the guided 71%–72% bottom in fiscal Q4 2027 and 72%–73% in fiscal 2028.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal year 2028 (ending January 2028), with fiscal 2027's fourth quarter as the guided trough; settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 quarterly results and Form 10-K gross margin disclosures, expected around February 2028.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-fy2027-revenue\",\n          \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027) revenue come in around the analysts' consensus of about $412 billion, roughly 90% growth, given fiscal Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and fiscal Q3 guidance of $108 billion?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2027 total revenue versus the ~$412 billion consensus and the ~90% year-over-year growth expectation.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal year 2027 (ending late January 2027); settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter earnings release and Form 10-K (annual report), expected around February 2027.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"kept_statements\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n          \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (period ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and a faster pace than the fiscal first quarter's 85% growth; the data center business accounted for $89 billion of the total, and net income was $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-fy2027-revenue\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"Reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion (+106% year over year), with $89 billion from data center and $59.7 billion of net income, is the actual base against which the fiscal 2027 full-year consensus (~$412 billion, ~90% growth) the thesis tracks will be measured.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n          \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, around 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-fy2027-revenue\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"Company guidance of $108 billion for fiscal Q3 2027, ~12% above fiscal Q2, is a direct input to whether fiscal 2027 revenue reaches the ~$412 billion consensus the thesis tracks.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n          \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and characterised the outlook as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-fy2028-revenue\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"This is the company's own guidance for fiscal 2028 revenue growth (~70%) that the thesis tracks, together with management's supply-constrained framing, which says the constraint on that growth is manufacturing capacity rather than demand.\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n              \"new\": false,\n              \"why\": \"A supply-constrained outlook implies NVIDIA must secure manufacturing capacity and components ahead of demand, which bears indirectly on how large the purchase and supply commitments disclosed at the next annual report will be; the item gives no commitment amount.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n          \"summary\": \"On the same late-August 2026 call, CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA's gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal 2027 fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in, a drop from the 75% gross margin of the fiscal 2027 second quarter.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-fy2028-gross-margin\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"Company guidance for the fiscal 2028 gross margin level (72%–73%), the fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter bottom (71%–72%) and the fiscal Q2 2027 actual (75%) are the figures the margin thesis tracks.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n          \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in at around $412 billion.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-fy2027-revenue\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"The ~$412 billion consensus is the sell-side estimate the thesis compares NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2027 revenue against.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A05\",\n      \"article\": 2,\n      \"new_questions\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"revenue-fy2027\",\n          \"question\": \"What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027, its current fiscal year?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027, compared with the Zacks consensus estimate of $406.05 billion cited in the 22 September 2026 item.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (current fiscal year ending late January 2027) — settled by NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 Form 10-K and fiscal-year earnings release, expected around February 2027.\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 Form 10-K and fiscal-year earnings release (total company revenue).\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"revenue-fy2028\",\n          \"question\": \"What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, its next fiscal year?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028, compared with the Zacks consensus estimate of $671.95 billion cited in the 22 September 2026 item.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028 (next fiscal year ending late January 2028) — settled by NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 Form 10-K and fiscal-year earnings release, expected around February 2028.\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 Form 10-K and fiscal-year earnings release (total company revenue).\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"kept_s