\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K / annual results for fiscal year 2027, which report total consolidated revenue.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-nvda-fy2027-gross-margin\",\n          \"question\": \"What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal year 2027?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 annual non-GAAP gross margin, compared with the fiscal Q2 2027 non-GAAP gross margin of 75% reported in this item.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal year 2027 (ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — current fiscal year; the annual report releases the annual gross margin.\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K / annual results for fiscal year 2027, which report annual gross margin (non-GAAP if disclosed).\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A48\",\n      \"article\": 5,\n      \"new_questions\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-gm-fy2027\",\n          \"question\": \"What gross margin will NVIDIA report for its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027)?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's reported annual gross margin for fiscal 2027, versus the 75% reported last quarter and analyst estimates of under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 results/Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — set as the release of that fiscal year's results for a [reason] quantity.\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's quarterly earnings releases and its fiscal 2027 Form 10-K, which report annual gross margin.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-rev-fy2027\",\n          \"question\": \"What total revenue growth will NVIDIA report for its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027)?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's reported annual total revenue growth for fiscal 2027, versus the roughly 90% or more growth expected in the article.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 results/Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — set as the release of that fiscal year's results for a [reason] quantity.\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 Form 10-K and earnings releases, which report annual revenue.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-rev-fy2028\",\n          \"question\": \"What total revenue growth will NVIDIA report for its next fiscal year (fiscal 2028)?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's reported annual total revenue growth for fiscal 2028, versus the company's 70% projection and the 45% consensus expectation cited at the time.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 results/Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — set as the release of that fiscal year's results for a [reason] quantity.\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 Form 10-K and earnings releases, which report annual revenue.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"kept_statements\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-gm-actual\",\n          \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter, qz.com reported, citing the company's results.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-gm-fy2027\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"The reported 75% gross margin is the starting point for tracking NVIDIA's annual fiscal 2027 gross margin.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-gm-estimate\",\n          \"summary\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg and cited by qz.com show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter from 75% last quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter.\",\n            \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-gm-fy2027\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"Forecast gross-margin compression to under 72% by the fourth quarter, driven by memory-component costs, informs the question on NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 gross margin.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-rev-netincome-fy2027\",\n          \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027), qz.com reported.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Nvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-rev-fy2027\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"The roughly 90%-or-more expected full-year revenue growth is a direct expectation for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-rev-fy2028-guidance\",\n          \"summary\": \"Following its second-quarter earnings, NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, qz.com reported.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-rev-fy2028\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"The company's 70% fiscal 2028 revenue-growth projection versus 45% consensus is the central forecast for NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 revenue question.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ev-q2-revenue-doubled\",\n          \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled in its second-quarter results, qz.com reported.\",\n          \"passages\": [\n            \"Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"\n          ],\n          \"linked_to\": [\n            {\n              \"question\": \"new-rev-fy2027\",\n              \"new\": true,\n              \"why\": \"The reported second-quarter revenue doubling is actual evidence on the trajectory of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue.\"\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A49\",\n      \"article\": 7,\n      \"new_questions\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"n1\",\n          \"question\": \"What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's full-year total revenue for fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), against the $96.2 billion Q2 run-rate and the company's own Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by NVIDIA's Q4/FY2027 results release and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter and full-year results release and Form 10-K.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"n2\",\n          \"question\": \"What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA's full-year non-GAAP gross margin for fiscal 2027, against the 75% reported in Q2 FY2027 and the approximately 74% (plus or minus 50 basis points) guided for Q3 FY2027.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by NVIDIA's Q4/FY2027 results release and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter and full-year results release and Form 10-K.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A50\",\n      \"article\": 7,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A51\",\n      \"article\": 7,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A52\",\n      \"article\": 4,\n      \"new_questions\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"new-supply-commitments\",\n          \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in supply commitments (commitments to secure supply/capacity, such as purchase obligations to suppliers) at its next annual report, given the report that it is taking on larger supply commitments?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of NVIDIA's supply-side commitments (purchase obligations/commitments to secure capacity, components or services) in its next annual report, versus prior periods.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K (fiscal 2027), including its contractual obligations / purchase and supply commitments disclosure.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A53\",\n      \"article\": 1,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A54\",\n      \"article\": 1,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A55\",\n      \"article\": 1,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A56\",\n      \"article\": 3,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A57\",\n      \"article\": 1,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A58\",\n      \"article\": 3,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A59\",\n      \"article\": 4,\n      \"new_questions\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"supply-commitments-1\",\n          \"question\": \"What amount of supply commitments (purchase obligations to buy capacity or services) will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n          \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of supply commitments / purchase obligations NVIDIA has taken on, as reported in its next annual report.\",\n          \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item.\",\n          \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's next Form 10-K, including its contractual obligations / purchase commitments disclosure.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"answer\": \"A60\",\n      \"article\": 1,\n      \"new_questions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/main-numbers/reader-packet.json\\\")); [print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"FACTS\\\",a.get(\\\"named_facts\\\",[])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a.get(\\\"named_facts\\\")]; [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"article\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]),[print(\\\"Q\\\",q[\\\"id\\\"],q[\\\"question\\\"],\\\"|\\\",q.get(\\\"outcome\\\"),\\\"|\\\",q.get(\\\"period\\\")) for q in a[\\\"new_questions\\\"]],[print(\\\"S\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"LINK\\\",[l[\\\"question\\\"] for l in s.get(\\\"linked_to\\\",[]) if l.get(\\\"new\\\")]) for s in a.get(\\\"kept_statements\\\",[])]) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/main-numbers/reader-packet.json\\\")); [print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"FACTS\\\",a.get(\\\"named_facts\\\",[])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a.get(\\\"named_facts\\\")]; [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"article\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]),[print(\\\"Q\\\",q[\\\"id\\\"],q[\\\"question\\\"],\\\"|\\\",q.get(\\\"outcome\\\"),\\\"|\\\",q.get(\\\"period\\\")) for q in a[\\\"new_questions\\\"]],[print(\\\"S\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"LINK\\\",[l[\\\"question\\\"] for l in s.get(\\\"linked_to\\\",[]) if l.get(\\\"new\\\")]) for s in a.get(\\\"kept_statements\\\",[])]) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 2 FACTS [{'fact': 1, 'text': 'the consensus sales estimate of $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year'}, {'fact': 2, 'text': 'the consensus sales estimate of $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year'}]\nARTICLE 5 FACTS [{'fact': 1, 'text': 'the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, against the 45% analysts had expected'}]\nARTICLE 8 FACTS [{'fact': 1, 'text': 'the Zacks consensus of $406.05 billion for fiscal 2027 revenues'}, {'fact': 2, 'text': 'the company expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%'}]\nARTICLE 9 FACTS [{'fact': 1, 'text': 'management guided about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028'}, {'fact': 2, 'text': 'the guide has the margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028'}]\nARTICLE 10 FACTS [{'fact': 1, 'text': \"analysts' consensus of about $412 billion for fiscal 2027 revenue\"}, {'fact': 2, 'text': 'the CFO expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028'}, {'fact': 3, 'text': 'gross margins to settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028'}]\n\n A01 article 4\nQ new-supply-commitments What total of supply commitments and purchase obligations will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report? | The disclosed amount of supply commitments/purchase obligations NVIDIA has taken on, as reported in its next annual report, and how it compares with prior disclosures. | Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\n\n A02 article 10\nS e1 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of fiscal Q1 — with the data center business accounting for $89 billion of the total, and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as reported in the company's quarterly results. LINK []\nS e2 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, which the article states is around 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue. LINK []\nS e3 On NVIDIA's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \"a supply-constrained outlook\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand. LINK []\nS e4 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said on the company's late-August earnings call that gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect, down from 75% in the fiscal second quarter. LINK []\nS e5 The article states that analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, and applying the guided roughly 70% growth to that figure derives fiscal 2028 revenue of somewhere around $700 billion. LINK []\n\n A03 article 2\nS ev1 Zacks reported that the consensus expects NVIDIA to post earnings of $2.47 per share in the current quarter, a +90% change from the year-ago quarter, and that the Zacks Consensus Estimate has risen +5.8% over the last 30 days. LINK []\nS ev2 Zacks reported that the consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's current fiscal year is $9.25 per share, implying a +93.9% change from the prior year, and that this estimate has risen +5.1% over the last 30 days. LINK []\nS ev3 Zacks reported that the consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's next fiscal year is $15.33 per share, a +65.8% change from what NVIDIA is expected to report a year earlier, and that this estimate has risen +21.8% over the past month. LINK []\nS ev4 Zacks reported that the consensus sales estimate for NVIDIA's current quarter is $109.02 billion, indicating a +91.2% year-over-year change. LINK []\nS ev5 Zacks reported that consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA are $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year, indicating changes of +88% and +65.5%, respectively. LINK []\n\n A04 article 10\nQ new-fy2028-revenue Will NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028) revenue grow roughly 70% year over year from fiscal 2027, as CFO Colette Kress guided on the late-August 2026 earnings call, or will the supply-constrained outlook produce a different total? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 total revenue and its growth rate versus fiscal 2027, compared with the roughly 70% growth the CFO guided (implying roughly $700 billion if the ~$412 billion fiscal 2027 consensus holds). | Fiscal year 2028 (ending January 2028); settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\nQ new-fy2028-gross-margin Where will NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 gross margin settle, versus the 72%–73% CFO Colette Kress guided and the 75% reported in fiscal 2027's second quarter? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin and the fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter trough, compared with the guided 71%–72% bottom in fiscal Q4 2027 and 72%–73% in fiscal 2028. | Fiscal year 2028 (ending January 2028), with fiscal 2027's fourth quarter as the guided trough; settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\nQ new-fy2027-revenue Will NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027) revenue come in around the analysts' consensus of about $412 billion, roughly 90% growth, given fiscal Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and fiscal Q3 guidance of $108 billion? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2027 total revenue versus the ~$412 billion consensus and the ~90% year-over-year growth expectation. | Fiscal year 2027 (ending late January 2027); settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\nS ev-1 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (period ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and a faster pace than the fiscal first quarter's 85% growth; the data center business accounted for $89 billion of the total, and net income was $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year. LINK ['new-fy2027-revenue']\nS ev-2 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, around 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion. LINK ['new-fy2027-revenue']\nS ev-3 On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and characterised the outlook as \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue']\nS ev-4 On the same late-August 2026 call, CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA's gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal 2027 fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in, a drop from the 75% gross margin of the fiscal 2027 second quarter. LINK ['new-fy2028-gross-margin']\nS ev-5 Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in at around $412 billion. LINK ['new-fy2027-revenue']\n\n A05 article 2\nQ revenue-fy2027 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027, its current fiscal year? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027, compared with the Zacks consensus estimate of $406.05 billion cited in the 22 September 2026 item. | Fiscal 2027 (current fiscal year ending late January 2027) — settled by NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 Form 10-K and fiscal-year earnings release, expected around February 2027.\nQ revenue-fy2028 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, its next fiscal year? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028, compared with the Zacks consensus estimate of $671.95 billion cited in the 22 September 2026 item. | Fiscal 2028 (next fiscal year ending late January 2028) — settled by NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 Form 10-K and fiscal-year earnings release, expected around February 2028.\nS e-eps-current-quarter Zacks reported that the consensus estimate for NVIDIA's current-quarter earnings was $2.47 per share, implying a +90% change from the year-ago quarter; it said the Zacks Consensus Estimate had changed +5.8% over the last 30 days. LINK []\nS e-eps-current-fy Zacks reported that the consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's current fiscal year was $9.25 per share, implying a +93.9% change from the prior year; it said the estimate had changed +5.1% over the last 30 days. LINK []\nS e-eps-next-fy Zacks reported that the consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's next fiscal year was $15.33 per share, implying a +65.8% change from what NVIDIA is expected to report a year earlier; it said the estimate had changed +21.8% over the past month. LINK []\nS e-sales-current-quarter Zacks reported that the consensus sales estimate for NVIDIA's current quarter was $109.02 billion, implying a +91.2% year-over-year change. LINK ['revenue-fy2027']\nS e-sales-current-next-fy Zacks reported consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA of $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year, indicating changes of +88% and +65.5%, respectively. LINK ['revenue-fy2027', 'revenue-fy2028']\n\n A06 article 10\nQ new-1 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027 (the year ending January 2027)? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027, versus the roughly $412 billion analysts' consensus cited in the item and the trajectory implied by fiscal Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and fiscal Q3 guidance of $108 billion. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3.\nQ new-2 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), given management's guidance of roughly 70% growth on a supply-constrained outlook? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028 versus the roughly 70% growth management guided, and whether the constraint proves to be manufacturing capacity rather than demand. | Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3.\nQ new-3 What gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), after management guided it to bottom at 71%–72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and settle at 72%–73% in fiscal 2028? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin versus the 72%–73% management guidance and the fiscal Q2 2027 level of 75%, and whether the contemplated price increases materialise. | Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3.\nS ev-1 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue (quarter ended July 26, 2026) of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth, with data center contributing $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-2 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, around 12% above its fiscal second-quarter revenue. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-3 On NVIDIA's late-August fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and called it \"a supply-constrained outlook\", with the limit being manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand. LINK ['new-2']\nS ev-4 On the same earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in, a small drop from the 75% gross margin reported in the fiscal second quarter. LINK ['new-3']\nS ev-5 Analysts' consensus had NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. LINK ['new-1']\n\n A07 article 9\nQ N1 Will NVIDIA report fiscal 2028 total revenue growth of about 70% year over year, as management has guided in its first full-year outlook? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 total revenue and year-over-year growth versus the ~70% management guidance. | Fiscal 2028 (fiscal year ending late January 2028); settled by NVIDIA's Q4 FY2028 results and FY2028 Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\nQ N2 What gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, against its guidance of a 72%–73% range as memory prices rise? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin versus the 72%–73% guided range, given higher-than-expected memory prices and price increases already executed. | Fiscal 2028 (fiscal year ending late January 2028); settled by NVIDIA's Q4 FY2028 results and FY2028 Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\nS e1 NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history and puts the revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. LINK ['N1']\nS e2 NVIDIA's Vera Rubin production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud provider and system OEM, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS e3 NVIDIA management guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70% year over year — the first time it has guided a full year ahead — and the CEO said demand is much greater than 70%, with supply, not demand, holding the guide at that level. LINK ['N1']\nS e4 NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. LINK ['N1', 'N2']\nS e5 NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower, to a 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices rose more than management expected, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. LINK ['N2']\nS e6 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management acknowledges some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers. LINK []\nS e7 The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year on which the forward multiple is priced. LINK []\nS e8 Trefis wrote that consensus expects NVIDIA's earnings and revenue to grow at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, implying analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. LINK ['N2']\n\n A08 article 9\nQ new-1 How fast will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in fiscal 2028 (the year ending late January 2028), against management's guidance of about 70% growth? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 total revenue and its year-over-year growth rate, compared with the roughly 70% growth management guided in September 2026. | Fiscal year 2028 (ending late January 2028), settled at the release of NVIDIA's FY2028 results and Form 10-K around February 2028 — set per section 3: a [reason] quantity is settled by the release of that fiscal year's results (the item gives no date of its own).\nQ new-2 Will NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 gross margin land in the 72%–73% range management guided, after memory prices rose more than expected? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin versus the 72%–73% range guided in September 2026. | Fiscal year 2028 (ending late January 2028), settled at the release of NVIDIA's FY2028 results and Form 10-K around February 2028 — a [reason] quantity settled by that fiscal year's results release.\nS e1 NVIDIA management said Vera Rubin production shipments began in August (2026), with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud provider and system OEM. The item does not state that revenue has been recognized on those shipments. LINK []\nS e2 NVIDIA management put the company's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of computing capacity with Vera Rubin, against about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell. LINK []\nS e3 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. Its CEO said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide at that level. LINK ['new-1']\nS e4 NVIDIA's CFO said supply will remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and described memory pricing as extreme. LINK ['new-1', 'new-2']\nS e5 NVIDIA guided its gross margin to settle in the 72%–73% range in fiscal 2028, after memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. LINK ['new-2']\nS e6 According to the Trefis article, consensus expects NVIDIA's earnings and revenue to grow at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, implying that analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. LINK ['new-2']\nS e7 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management acknowledged some will call this circular financing and said the compute can be redeployed to other customers. LINK []\nS e8 The 33 analyst estimates for NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on. LINK []\n\n A09 article 6\nQ new-nvda-fy27-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027? | NVIDIA's consolidated revenue for fiscal year 2027. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027, expected around February 2027, will report the annual figure.\nQ new-nvda-fy27-gross-margin What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027? | NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin for fiscal year 2027. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027, expected around February 2027, will report the annual gross margin and non-GAAP reconciliation.\n\n A10 article 8\nS e1 NVIDIA disclosed that at the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027 it had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities for current and future data center products. LINK []\nS e2 NVIDIA reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues of $96.22 billion, up 106% year over year, with Data Center revenues up 117% to $89.02 billion. LINK []\nS e3 NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which the article says implies 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. LINK []\nS e4 NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, according to the article. LINK []\nS e5 NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, while cautioning that supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK []\nS e6 The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenues stands at $406.05 billion, which the article says implies an 88% year-over-year increase. LINK []\nS e7 The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's earnings implies year-over-year increases of approximately 93.9% for fiscal 2027 and 65.8% for fiscal 2028, and both years' estimates were revised upward over the past 30 days. LINK []\nS e8 AMD's Data Center revenues rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion in the second quarter of 2026, with Instinct accelerator sales more than doubling, according to the article. LINK []\nS e9 AMD's Helios rack-scale AI platform is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter of 2026 and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and in 2027. LINK []\nS e10 AMD expects its Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027. LINK []\nS e11 Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of AMD MI450-series GPUs on the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. LINK []\nS e12 Microsoft plans to deploy AMD's Helios rack-scale platform at scale on Azure, according to the article. LINK []\nS e13 Intel's second-quarter 2026 revenues rose 25% to $16.13 billion, with Data Center and AI revenues up 59% to $6.26 billion. LINK []\nS e14 Intel's purpose-built silicon revenues rose about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year in the second quarter of 2026; on the last earnings call management said purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate and that the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future. LINK []\n\n A11 article 10\nQ new-nvda-fy2027-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027, the year ending late January 2027? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027, compared with the fiscal Q2 actual of $96.2 billion, fiscal Q3 guidance of $108 billion and analysts' consensus of about $412 billion. | NVIDIA fiscal 2027 annual results (Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the release of that fiscal year's results, per [reason].\nQ new-nvda-fy2028-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028, compared with the CFO's expectation of roughly 70% growth and the article's calculation of about $700 billion. | NVIDIA fiscal 2028 annual results (Form 10-K expected around February 2028) — the release of that fiscal year's results, per [reason].\nQ new-nvda-fy2028-gross-margin What gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin, compared with the CFO's expectation that it settles at 72% to 73%, after bottoming at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and versus 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. | NVIDIA fiscal 2028 annual results (Form 10-K expected around February 2028) — the release of that fiscal year's results, per [reason].\nS e1 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year earlier and faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth, with the data center business contributing $89 billion; net income was $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, for the quarter ended July 26, 2026. LINK ['new-nvda-fy2027-revenue', 'new-nvda-fy2028-revenue']\nS e2 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, which the article says is around 12% above fiscal Q2. LINK ['new-nvda-fy2027-revenue', 'new-nvda-fy2028-revenue']\nS e3 On the late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and called it \"a supply-constrained outlook\"; the article paraphrased this as manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, being the limit. LINK ['new-nvda-fy2028-revenue']\nS e4 In this article, analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, and the article elsewhere refers to \"this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth\"; applying the CFO's roughly 70% growth rate implies fiscal 2028 revenue of somewhere around $700 billion. LINK ['new-nvda-fy2027-revenue', 'new-nvda-fy2028-revenue']\nS e5 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said on the call that gross margins should bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in; the article notes this compares with fiscal Q2 2027 gross margin of 75%. LINK ['new-nvda-fy2028-gross-margin']\n\n A12 article 5\nS e1 Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, even as the stock trades near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to expectations reported by qz.com citing Bloomberg. The actor is the consensus expectation (unnamed forecasters) as reported by the outlet; it is a forecast for the current fiscal year, not a reported result. LINK []\nS e2 Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in its most recent quarter, and analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor. Reported actual versus forecast are distinct: 75% is a reported result, the sub-72% figure is an estimate. LINK []\nS e3 Nvidia's revenue more than doubled year over year in its most recent quarter, and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, according to qz.com. The 70% figure is company guidance; the 45% figure was the prior consensus. LINK []\nS e4 David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, said the growing threat of customers building their own silicon — noting that Meta and Alphabet are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally — will gradually erode Nvidia's pricing power and market share. This is his opinion; the item gives no date, volume or contract detail for the in-house chip efforts. LINK []\n\n A13 article 2\nS ev-1 The Zacks Consensus Estimate projects NVIDIA will post earnings of $2.47 per share in the current quarter, a change of +90% from the year-ago quarter; the consensus figure itself moved +5.8% over the last 30 days. LINK []\nS ev-2 The Zacks Consensus Estimate projects NVIDIA earnings of $9.25 per share for the current fiscal year, a change of +93.9% from the prior year, with the estimate up +5.1% over the last 30 days. LINK []\nS ev-3 The Zacks Consensus Estimate projects NVIDIA earnings of $15.33 per share for the next fiscal year, a change of +65.8% from what NVIDIA is expected to report a year earlier, and the estimate rose +21.8% over the past month. LINK []\nS ev-4 The consensus sales estimate for NVIDIA's current quarter is $109.02 billion, a year-over-year change of +91.2%. LINK []\nS ev-5 Consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA are $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year, indicating changes of +88% and +65.5% respectively. LINK []\n\n A14 article 6\n\n A15 article 5\nQ nt-1 How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in fiscal 2027, against the roughly 90%-or-more growth consensus reported in this item? | NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 total revenue growth versus fiscal 2026, against the roughly 90%-or-more consensus expectation. | Fiscal 2027 results release (Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the current fiscal year's annual results.\nQ nt-2 Will NVIDIA's total revenue grow about 70% in fiscal 2028, as the company projected? | NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 total revenue growth versus fiscal 2027, against the company's 70% projection. | Fiscal 2028 results release (Form 10-K expected around February 2028) — the next fiscal year's annual results.\nQ nt-3 What will NVIDIA's gross margin be for fiscal 2027, against the 75% reported last quarter and analyst estimates of sub-72% by the fourth quarter? | NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 annual gross margin and its quarterly trajectory, against the 75% reported last quarter and the sub-72% fourth-quarter estimate. | Fiscal 2027 annual report (Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the current fiscal year's annual gross margin and fourth-quarter result.\nS ev-1 NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, according to Quartz's report on the company's second-quarter earnings. LINK ['nt-2']\nS ev-2 NVIDIA's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027), according to expectations reported by Quartz. LINK ['nt-1']\nS ev-3 NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in its most recently reported quarter ('last quarter'), according to Quartz. LINK ['nt-3']\nS ev-4 Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor, according to Quartz. LINK ['nt-3']\nS ev-5 NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled in its second quarter, according to Quartz's report on the earnings that preceded the fiscal 2028 growth projection. LINK ['nt-1']\n\n A16 article 1\n\n A17 article 3\n\n A18 article 2\nQ rev_fy27 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for its current fiscal year (the fiscal year ending late January 2027)? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for the current fiscal year, to be compared with the $406.05 billion consensus sales estimate cited in the 22 September 2026 Zacks article. | Current fiscal year (FY2027, ending late January 2027) — set from the item's 'current fiscal year' consensus estimate; settled by NVIDIA's release of that fiscal year's results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027.\nQ rev_fy28 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for its next fiscal year (the fiscal year ending late January 2028)? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for the next fiscal year, to be compared with the $671.95 billion consensus sales estimate cited in the 22 September 2026 Zacks article. | Next fiscal year (FY2028, ending late January 2028) — set from the item's 'next fiscal year' consensus estimate; settled by the release of that fiscal year's results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028.\nS e1 Zacks reported that, as of its 22 September 2026 article, the consensus expects NVIDIA to post earnings of $2.47 per share in the current quarter, a +90% change from the year-ago quarter, and that the Zacks Consensus Estimate for that quarter had changed +5.8% over the prior 30 days. LINK []\nS e2 Zacks reported that the consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's current fiscal year was $9.25 per share, pointing to a +93.9% change from the prior year, and that the estimate had changed +5.1% over the prior 30 days. LINK []\nS e3 Zacks reported that the consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's next fiscal year was $15.33 per share, indicating a +65.8% change from what NVIDIA is expected to report a year earlier, and that the estimate had changed +21.8% over the past month. LINK []\nS e4 Zacks reported consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA of $109.02 billion for the current quarter (+91.2% year over year) and, for the current and next fiscal years, $406.05 billion (+88%) and $671.95 billion (+65.5%), respectively, as of its 22 September 2026 article. LINK ['rev_fy27', 'rev_fy28']\n\n A19 article 3\n\n A20 article 9\nS e1 NVIDIA management said it expects Vera Rubin, its newest AI platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, putting NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin versus $25 billion with Blackwell. LINK []\nS e2 NVIDIA's Vera Rubin production shipments began in August 2026, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud provider and system OEM, according to the article. LINK []\nS e3 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth to about 70%, the first time it has guided a full year ahead; CEO Jensen Huang said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there. LINK []\nS e4 NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and called memory pricing extreme. LINK []\nS e5 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2028 gross margin to settle in the 72% to 73% range, lower, as memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. LINK []\nS e6 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management acknowledges some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers. LINK []\nS e7 The 33 analyst estimates for NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings per share range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share. LINK []\nS e8 Consensus expects NVIDIA's earnings and revenue to grow at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, implying analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. LINK []\n\n A21 article 4\n\n A22 article 5\nQ new-1 What revenue growth will Nvidia report for fiscal 2028, and what revenue level does it imply, against the 70% growth the company guided to at its fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings? | Nvidia's reported fiscal 2028 revenue growth (and resulting revenue), versus the 70% growth management projected in September 2026 and the ~45% growth analysts had expected before that guidance. | Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled by the fiscal 2028 Form 10-K expected around February 2028, per section 3 ([reason] quantity for the next fiscal year, settled by the release of that year's results).\nQ new-2 What gross margin will Nvidia report for fiscal 2027, after 75% in the second quarter and analyst estimates of under 72% by the fourth quarter on rising memory-component costs? | Nvidia's reported fiscal 2027 gross margin versus the 75% second-quarter level and the under-72% fourth-quarter analyst estimate. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by the fiscal 2027 Form 10-K expected around February 2027, per section 3 ([reason] quantity for the current fiscal year, settled by the release of that year's results).\nS ev-1 Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027), according to estimates cited by the article, even as the stock trades near its lowest valuation in more than a decade. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-2 Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the quarter before the article ('last quarter', its fiscal 2027 second quarter). LINK ['new-2']\nS ev-3 Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show Nvidia's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; the article cites rising costs for key components such as memory chips as a central factor. LINK ['new-2']\nS ev-4 Nvidia guided to 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 when it reported second-quarter earnings, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-5 Nvidia's revenue more than doubled year over year in the second quarter reported with its second-quarter earnings. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-6 David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, said Meta Platforms and Alphabet are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally, a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia's pricing power and market share. LINK ['new-1', 'new-2']\n\n A23 article 10\nS e1 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended 26 July 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth — with the data center business accounting for $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, according to the company's latest results as reported by fool.com. LINK []\nS e2 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, around 12% above the $96.2 billion reported for fiscal 2027's second quarter, as reported by fool.com. LINK []\nS e3 On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), calling it \"a supply-constrained outlook\" — i.e., the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand, as reported by fool.com. LINK []\nS e4 On the same late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in, a drop from the 75% gross margin reported for the fiscal 2027 second quarter, as reported by fool.com. LINK []\nS e5 Analysts' consensus, as cited by fool.com, had NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion. LINK []\n\n A24 article 6\nQ new-1 Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin data-center platform — the successor generation of the product line that carries the company's data-center value — reach first customer shipments by the end of fiscal 2027? | First revenue shipments of the Vera Rubin platform, after the company reported it entered full production, confirmed by NVIDIA's own shipment/revenue commentary in quarterly results and SEC filings. | NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date because the item reports full production but gives no shipment date.\n\n A25 article 7\n\n A26 article 8\nQ new-1 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027 (the year ending late January 2027)? | NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 total revenue, versus the Zacks consensus of $406.05 billion (+88% year over year), given second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion (+106% year over year, Data Center $89.02 billion, +117%) and third-quarter guidance of $108 billion +/- 2%. | Fiscal year 2027 (ending late January 2027); settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027.\nS e1 NVIDIA reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year over year, with Data Center revenue up 117% to $89.02 billion. LINK ['new-1']\nS e2 NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which the article says implies 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. LINK ['new-1']\nS e3 NVIDIA said it expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, while cautioning that supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK ['new-1']\nS e4 NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity at the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. LINK []\nS e5 NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter (of fiscal 2027), per the article. LINK ['new-1']\nS e6 Advanced Micro Devices reported second-quarter 2026 Data Center revenue of $6.7 billion, up 107% year over year, with Instinct accelerator sales more than doubling. LINK []\nS e7 AMD said its Helios rack-scale platform is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter of 2026 and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027. LINK []\nS e8 Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of AMD MI450-series GPUs using the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. LINK []\nS e9 Microsoft plans to deploy AMD's Helios rack-scale platform at scale on Azure. LINK []\nS e10 AMD expects its Data Center segment revenue to more than double year over year in 2027. LINK []\nS e11 Intel reported second-quarter 2026 revenue of $16.13 billion, up 25%, with Data Center and AI revenue up 59% to $6.26 billion. LINK []\nS e12 Intel's purpose-built silicon revenue rose about 20% sequentially in the second quarter of 2026 and nearly tripled year over year. LINK []\nS e13 On its last earnings call, Intel management said purpose-built silicon revenue is approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate and that the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future. LINK []\nS e14 The Zacks Consensus Estimate put NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at $406.05 billion, an 88% year-over-year increase. LINK ['new-1']\nS e15 The Zacks Consensus Estimate implies NVIDIA's earnings will grow about 93.9% year over year in fiscal 2027 and 65.8% in fiscal 2028, and the consensus estimates for both fiscal years were revised upward over the past 30 days. LINK []\n\n A27 article 7\n\n A28 article 9\nS ev-1 NVIDIA management says Vera Rubin, its newest AI platform, should be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and production shipments of the platform began in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud provider and system OEM, according to Trefis's 24 September 2026 article. LINK []\nS ev-2 NVIDIA management puts the company's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell, per Trefis. LINK []\nS ev-3 NVIDIA management guided about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, the first time it has given a full-year-ahead guide; the CEO said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide at that level, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS ev-4 NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS ev-5 NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower, to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS ev-6 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS ev-7 The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share — a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on, according to Trefis. LINK []\n\n A29 article 10\nS ev1 NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (quarter ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% growth; the data center business accounted for $89 billion of the total, and net income was $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year. Reported by fool.com (Daniel Sparks) citing NVIDIA's results. LINK []\nS ev2 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, about 12% above second-quarter revenue, according to the article. LINK []\nS ev3 On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), calling it \"a supply-constrained outlook\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand. LINK []\nS ev4 On the earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect, down from 75% in the fiscal second quarter. LINK []\nS ev5 Analysts' consensus had NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, according to the article. LINK []\n\n A30 article 8\nQ new-fy2027-total-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027? | NVIDIA's total revenue for fiscal 2027, compared with the $96.22 billion reported in Q2 fiscal 2027, the $108 billion Q3 fiscal 2027 guidance and the $406.05 billion Zacks Consensus Estimate. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), settled by NVIDIA's FY2027 Form 10-K expected around February 2027.\nQ new-fy2028-revenue-growth What year-over-year revenue growth will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028? | NVIDIA's total revenue growth for fiscal 2028, compared with the company's expectation of about 70% growth. | Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028), settled by NVIDIA's FY2028 Form 10-K expected around February 2028.\nS e1_279b_commitments NVIDIA reported that, at the end of Q2 fiscal 2027, it had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion at the end of Q1 fiscal 2027. Most of the commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. LINK []\nS e2_q2_fy2027_revenue NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year over year, including Data Center revenue of $89.02 billion, up 117%. LINK ['new-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e3_q3_fy2027_guidance NVIDIA guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, implying 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. LINK ['new-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e4_vera_rubin_shipments NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in Q3 fiscal 2027, according to the article. LINK []\nS e5_fy2028_revenue_growth_guidance NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e6_zacks_fy2027_revenue The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue is $406.05 billion, implying an 88% year-over-year increase. LINK ['new-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e7_zacks_fy2027_fy2028_eps The Zacks Consensus Estimate implies NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028 earnings will grow approximately 93.9% and 65.8% year over year, respectively; Zacks says estimates for both years were revised upward over the past 30 days. LINK []\nS e8_amd_q2_2026_dc_revenue AMD reported Q2 2026 Data Center revenue of $6.7 billion, up 107% year over year, and said Instinct sales more than doubled. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e9_amd_helios_production AMD said its Helios rack-scale platform is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e10_anthropic_amd_deployment Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of AMD MI450-series GPUs using AMD's Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e11_microsoft_amd_helios Microsoft plans to deploy AMD's Helios platform at scale on Azure. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e12_amd_dc_guidance_2027 AMD expects its Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e13_intel_q2_2026_revenue Intel reported Q2 2026 revenue of $16.13 billion, up 25%, including Data Center and AI revenue of $6.26 billion, up 59%. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e14_intel_purpose_built_actual Intel reported that Q2 2026 purpose-built silicon revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e15_intel_purpose_built_outlook On its last earnings call, Intel management said purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate and that the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\n\n A31 article 8\nQ nvda-fy2027-total-revenue How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027, the year that carries the business? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal year 2027 (year ending late January 2027), judged against the Zacks consensus of $406.05 billion and the run-rate implied by $96.22 billion of second-quarter revenue and $108 billion third-quarter guidance. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by NVIDIA's fourth-quarter and full-year fiscal 2027 results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 for a [reason] quantity.\nS e1-supply-commitments NVIDIA reported that at the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027 it had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. LINK []\nS e2-q2-fy2027-revenue NVIDIA reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year over year, with Data Center revenue up 117% to $89.02 billion. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e3-q3-fy2027-guidance NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which the article says implies 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e4-vera-rubin-production-shipments NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform began production shipments in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, the article reports. LINK []\nS e5-fy2028-revenue-growth-outlook NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the article reports, adding that supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK []\nS e6-zacks-fy2027-revenue-consensus The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue is $406.05 billion, implying an 88% year-over-year increase, Zacks reported. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e7-amd-q2-2026-data-center-revenue AMD's Data Center revenue rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion in the second quarter of 2026, with Instinct accelerator sales more than doubling, the article reports. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e8-amd-helios-shipment-plan AMD's Helios rack-scale AI platform is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter of 2026 and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027, the article reports. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e9-anthropic-mi450-deployment Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of AMD MI450-series GPUs using AMD's Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e10-microsoft-helios-azure Microsoft plans to deploy AMD's Helios platform at scale on Azure, the article reports. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e11-amd-2027-data-center-outlook AMD expects its Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e12-intel-q2-2026-revenue Intel's second-quarter 2026 revenue rose 25% to $16.13 billion, with Data Center and AI revenue up 59% to $6.26 billion, the article reports. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e13-intel-purpose-built-silicon Intel management said on its last earnings call that purpose-built silicon revenue, which rose about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year in the second quarter of 2026, is approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate, with the company targeting $4 billion in the near future. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\nS e14-zacks-eps-consensus-and-revisions The Zacks Consensus Estimate implies NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028 earnings will rise approximately 93.9% and 65.8% year over year respectively, and estimates for both years were revised upward over the past 30 days, Zacks reported. LINK []\n\n A32 article 4\n\n A33 article 3\n\n A34 article 6\nQ new-1 Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform — the next-generation data-center GPU system that carries the company's value — reach first customer shipments (revenue-generating deliveries) by the end of fiscal 2027? | Whether NVIDIA confirms first customer shipments, and revenue, from Vera Rubin systems; the item reports the platform entered full production in the period covered by the Aug. 26, 2026 press release (fiscal Q2 2027). | NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date, since the item reports full-production entry but gives no shipment date of its own.\n\n A35 article 2\nQ new-fy27-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027, ending late January 2027), versus the Zacks consensus of $406.05 billion (+88% year over year) as of 22 September 2026? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027 and its growth versus fiscal 2026, compared with the consensus estimate of $406.05 billion (+88%). | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter and full-year results, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\nQ new-fy28-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for its next fiscal year (fiscal 2028, ending late January 2028), versus the Zacks consensus of $671.95 billion (+65.5%) as of 22 September 2026? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028 and its growth versus fiscal 2027, compared with the consensus estimate of $671.95 billion (+65.5%). | Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled by the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter and full-year results, expected around February 2029 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\nS ev-q-eps Zacks' consensus estimate expects NVIDIA to report earnings of $2.47 per share in the current quarter (the quarter current as of the 22 September 2026 article), a change of +90% from the year-ago quarter; the Zacks Consensus Estimate for that quarter has risen 5.8% over the last 30 days. LINK []\nS ev-fy27-eps Zacks' consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's current fiscal year (fiscal 2027, ending late January 2027) is $9.25 per share, a change of +93.9% from the prior year, and the estimate has risen 5.1% over the last 30 days. LINK []\nS ev-fy28-eps Zacks' consensus earnings estimate for NVIDIA's next fiscal year (fiscal 2028, ending late January 2028) is $15.33 per share, a change of +65.8% from what NVIDIA is expected to report a year earlier, and the estimate has risen 21.8% over the past month. LINK []\nS ev-q-sales Zacks' consensus sales estimate for NVIDIA's current quarter (the quarter current as of the 22 September 2026 article) is $109.02 billion, a year-over-year change of +91.2%. LINK ['new-fy27-revenue']\nS ev-annual-sales Zacks' consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA are $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year (fiscal 2027) and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year (fiscal 2028), indicating changes of +88% and +65.5% respectively. LINK ['new-fy27-revenue', 'new-fy28-revenue']\n\n A36 article 9\nS ev1 NVIDIA management says Vera Rubin, its newest AI platform, carries a revenue opportunity of about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity versus about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell, and expects Vera Rubin to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS ev2 NVIDIA began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, according to the company as reported by Trefis. LINK []\nS ev3 NVIDIA management guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the first time it has given a full-year guide; CEO says demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there, while the CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. LINK []\nS ev4 NVIDIA guided its gross margin to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lower as memory costs climb; memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS ev5 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs; management acknowledges some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS ev6 The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings per share range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year on which the forward multiple is priced, according to Trefis. LINK []\n\n A37 article 7\nQ new-1 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027 (the fiscal year ending late January 2027)? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027 versus the prior year, as disclosed in its FY2027 annual results and Form 10-K; the item supports a Q2 FY2027 actual of $96.2 billion and Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion (±2%, excluding China data-center compute revenue). | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by NVIDIA's FY2027 annual results / Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason] quantity).\n\n A38 article 5\nS ev-1 As reported by qz.com (with market data attributed to Bloomberg), Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, even as the stock trades near its lowest valuation in more than a decade. The expectations are unattributed consensus figures rather than company guidance. LINK []\nS ev-2 Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the prior quarter, but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor in the compression. LINK []\nS ev-3 At its second-quarter earnings, Nvidia's revenue more than doubled year-over-year and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time. The stock rose roughly 7% after the report. LINK []\nS ev-4 David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, pointed to the growing threat of Nvidia customers building their own silicon, noting that Meta and Alphabet are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally; he believes this shift will gradually erode Nvidia's pricing power and market share. The in-house chip programmes are described as already under way, with no dates or volumes given. LINK []\n\n A39 article 2\nS ev-1 Zacks' consensus estimates project NVIDIA earnings of $2.47 per share for the current quarter (a +90% change from the year-ago quarter), $9.25 for the current fiscal year (+93.9% versus the prior year) and $15.33 for the next fiscal year (+65.8% versus what NVIDIA is expected to report a year ago). Over the last 30 days the Zacks Consensus Estimate changed +5.8% for the current quarter, +5.1% for the current fiscal year and +21.8% for the next fiscal year. LINK []\nS ev-2 Zacks reports consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA of $109.02 billion for the current quarter (a year-over-year change of +91.2%), $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year (+88%) and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year (+65.5%). LINK []\n\n A40 article 4\n\n A41 article 8\nS e1 At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter; most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. LINK []\nS e2 NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion. LINK []\nS e3 NVIDIA expects third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues of $108 billion (+/- 2%), indicating 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. LINK []\nS e4 NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform began production shipments in the third quarter (fiscal 2027), according to the article. LINK []\nS e5 NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although it notes supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK []\nS e6 The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenues is $406.05 billion, indicating an 88% year-over-year increase. LINK []\nS e7 The Zacks Consensus Estimate implies NVIDIA earnings per share growth of approximately 93.9% for fiscal 2027 and 65.8% for fiscal 2028 year over year, and both fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028 estimates were revised upward over the past 30 days. LINK []\nS e8 In the second quarter of 2026, AMD's Data Center revenues rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion while Instinct sales more than doubled; AMD's Helios rack-scale platform is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027. LINK []\nS e9 Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of MI450-series GPUs using AMD's Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. LINK []\nS e10 Microsoft plans to deploy AMD's Helios rack-scale platform at scale on Azure. LINK []\nS e11 Advanced Micro Devices expects its Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027. LINK []\nS e12 Intel's second-quarter revenues rose 25% to $16.13 billion, while its Data Center and AI revenues surged 59% to $6.26 billion. LINK []\nS e13 In the second quarter of 2026 Intel's purpose-built silicon revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year; on its last earnings call management said these revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate and it is targeting $4 billion in the near future. LINK []\n\n A42 article 8\nS ev-1 NVIDIA disclosed that at the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027 it had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter; most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, as NVIDIA ramps Blackwell and Vera Rubin. LINK []\nS ev-2 NVIDIA warns that changing demand could make its supply commitments difficult to reduce, and that it could face higher costs or excess inventory if customer demand slows or product transitions occur faster than expected. LINK []\nS ev-3 NVIDIA reported second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year over year, with Data Center revenue up 117% to $89.02 billion. LINK []\nS ev-4 NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which the item says implies 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. LINK []\nS ev-5 NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, though the item notes supply constraints could limit how quickly the company meets demand. LINK []\nS ev-6 NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter (fiscal 2027), as the company ramps Blackwell and Vera Rubin, according to the article. LINK []\nS ev-7 The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue is $406.05 billion, which the item says indicates an 88% year-over-year increase. LINK []\nS ev-8 The Zacks Consensus Estimate implies NVIDIA earnings per share growth of approximately 93.9% in fiscal 2027 and 65.8% in fiscal 2028, and the estimates for both years were revised upward over the past 30 days. LINK []\nS ev-9 Advanced Micro Devices reported second-quarter 2026 Data Center revenue of $6.7 billion, up 107% year over year, with Instinct accelerator sales more than doubling. LINK []\nS ev-10 AMD said its Helios rack-scale platform is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter of 2026 and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and in 2027. LINK []\nS ev-11 Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of AMD MI450-series GPUs using AMD's Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. LINK []\nS ev-12 Microsoft plans to deploy AMD's Helios rack-scale platform at scale on Azure. LINK []\nS ev-13 AMD expects its Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027. LINK []\nS ev-14 Intel reported second-quarter 2026 revenues of $16.13 billion, up 25%, with Data Center and AI revenues up 59% to $6.26 billion. LINK []\nS ev-15 Intel's purpose-built silicon (custom chip) revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year in the second quarter of 2026. LINK []\nS ev-16 Intel management said on its last earnings call that purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate and that the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future. LINK []\n\n A43 article 9\nQ new-1 What total revenue growth will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 total revenue and its year-over-year growth rate, versus management's guide of about 70% growth (and against the supply constraint the CFO describes). | Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled by NVIDIA's FY2028 fourth-quarter and full-year results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason] quantity).\nQ new-2 What gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028? | NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin versus the guided 72%–73% range, and whether memory cost inflation is offset by the price increases the company has already executed. | Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled by NVIDIA's FY2028 fourth-quarter and full-year results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason] quantity).\nS ev-1 NVIDIA management put the revenue opportunity from its Vera Rubin platform at about $40 billion per gigawatt of computing capacity, against about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell, as reported by Trefis on 24 September 2026. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-2 NVIDIA's Vera Rubin production shipments began in August (2026), with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, as reported by Trefis on 24 September 2026. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-3 NVIDIA has guided to about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, the first time it has given a full-year-ahead guide, according to Trefis's 24 September 2026 article. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-4 NVIDIA's CEO said demand is much greater than the 70% growth in the fiscal 2028 revenue guide and that supply is what holds the guide there; the CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, per Trefis's 24 September 2026 article. LINK ['new-1']\nS ev-5 NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, and management concedes some will call this circular financing while saying the compute can be redeployed to other customers, as reported by Trefis on 24 September 2026. LINK []\nS ev-6 The 33 analyst estimates for NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings range from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a band Trefis calls wide for the year the forward multiple is priced on, in its 24 September 2026 article. LINK []\nS ev-7 Trefis said consensus has NVIDIA's earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, implying analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady, and that for the forecast to hold the lost gross margin would have to be made up further down the income statement. LINK ['new-2']\nS ev-8 NVIDIA guides its fiscal 2028 gross margin to settle in the 72% to 73% range, lower than before, because memory prices have risen more than management expected, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed, per Trefis's 24 September 2026 article. LINK ['new-2']\n\n A44 article 3\n\n A45 article 5\nS e1 According to the qz.com article, Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, even as the stock trades near its lowest valuation in more than a decade. The expectation is reported without a named forecaster. LINK []\nS e2 Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the quarter the article calls 'last quarter', the quarter preceding the 22 September 2026 publication. LINK []\nS e3 Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg, cited by qz.com, show Nvidia's gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; the article names rising costs for key components such as memory chips as a central factor. LINK []\nS e4 David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, said that Meta and Alphabet are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally, a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia's pricing power and market share. LINK []\nS e5 Nvidia's revenue more than doubled in its second quarter, according to the article, which also notes the stock climbed roughly 7% after that earnings report. LINK []\nS e6 In its second-quarter earnings report, Nvidia projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time. LINK []\n\n A46 article 6\nQ new-1 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal year 2027 (the fiscal year ending late January 2027)? | NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027 versus fiscal 2026, with the Data Center segment as the segment that carries the business. | Release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 full-year results (Form 10-K and fourth-quarter/full-year press release, expected around February 2027) — set as the release of that fiscal year's results per section 3 for a [reason] quantity.\n\n A47 article 6\nQ new-nvda-fy2027-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal year 2027? | NVIDIA's total consolidated revenue for fiscal 2027, as reported in its annual results, against the fiscal Q2 2027 actual and fiscal Q3 2027 guidance in this item. | Fiscal year 2027 (ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — current fiscal year supported by the item's fiscal Q2 2027 results and fiscal Q3 2027 guidance; the annual report releases the annual figure.\nQ new-nvda-fy2027-gross-margin What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal year 2027? | NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 annual non-GAAP gross margin, compared with the fiscal Q2 2027 non-GAAP gross margin of 75% reported in this item. | Fiscal year 2027 (ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — current fiscal year; the annual report releases the annual gross margin.\n\n A48 article 5\nQ new-gm-fy2027 What gross margin will NVIDIA report for its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027)? | NVIDIA's reported annual gross margin for fiscal 2027, versus the 75% reported last quarter and analyst estimates of under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 results/Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — set as the release of that fiscal year's results for a [reason] quantity.\nQ new-rev-fy2027 What total revenue growth will NVIDIA report for its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027)? | NVIDIA's reported annual total revenue growth for fiscal 2027, versus the roughly 90% or more growth expected in the article. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 results/Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — set as the release of that fiscal year's results for a [reason] quantity.\nQ new-rev-fy2028 What total revenue growth will NVIDIA report for its next fiscal year (fiscal 2028)? | NVIDIA's reported annual total revenue growth for fiscal 2028, versus the company's 70% projection and the 45% consensus expectation cited at the time. | Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 results/Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 — set as the release of that fiscal year's results for a [reason] quantity.\nS ev-gm-actual NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter, qz.com reported, citing the company's results. LINK ['new-gm-fy2027']\nS ev-gm-estimate Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg and cited by qz.com show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter from 75% last quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor. LINK ['new-gm-fy2027']\nS ev-rev-netincome-fy2027 NVIDIA's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027), qz.com reported. LINK ['new-rev-fy2027']\nS ev-rev-fy2028-guidance Following its second-quarter earnings, NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, qz.com reported. LINK ['new-rev-fy2028']\nS ev-q2-revenue-doubled NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled in its second-quarter results, qz.com reported. LINK ['new-rev-fy2027']\n\n A49 article 7\nQ n1 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027? | NVIDIA's full-year total revenue for fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), against the $96.2 billion Q2 run-rate and the company's own Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by NVIDIA's Q4/FY2027 results release and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\nQ n2 What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027? | NVIDIA's full-year non-GAAP gross margin for fiscal 2027, against the 75% reported in Q2 FY2027 and the approximately 74% (plus or minus 50 basis points) guided for Q3 FY2027. | Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by NVIDIA's Q4/FY2027 results release and Form 10-K, expected around February 2027 — the release of that fiscal year's results, per section 3 ([reason]).\n\n A50 article 7\n\n A51 article 7\n\n A52 article 4\nQ new-supply-commitments How much will NVIDIA disclose in supply commitments (commitments to secure supply/capacity, such as purchase obligations to suppliers) at its next annual report, given the report that it is taking on larger supply commitments? | The disclosed amount of NVIDIA's supply-side commitments (purchase obligations/commitments to secure capacity, components or services) in its next annual report, versus prior periods. | Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\n\n A53 article 1\n\n A54 article 1\n\n A55 article 1\n\n A56 article 3\n\n A57 article 1\n\n A58 article 3\n\n A59 article 4\nQ supply-commitments-1 What amount of supply commitments (purchase obligations to buy capacity or services) will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report? | The disclosed amount of supply commitments / purchase obligations NVIDIA has taken on, as reported in its next annual report. | Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item.\n\n A60 article 1\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\").get_text(\\\" \\\",strip=True); x=s.find(\\\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\\\"); print(s[x:x+5000])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\").get_text(\\\" \\\",strip=True); x=s.find(\\\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\\\"); print(s[x:x+5000])'\"","aggregated_output":"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判 D248 。 另有一条前提：新问题要有独立的投资判断；一个新产品、一个新数字、一个更窄的范围，单凭这些不开 D201 。 四个测试 第 1 版 §6 值得追 写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判 D248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品 ；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。 独立意义 能写出两个情景吗：其他问题的答案不变，只有这一题相反，而对业务的判断确实不同？写不出就优先作为证据 评审 §1 第 3、8 条 。评审里的一个判例：NVIDIA FY2027 总收入题判为数据中心收入题的证据，理由是\"该段明确说主要增长来自数据中心，未提出独立的其他业务争论\"。这是对那一段材料的判断，别的材料要逐个判。 能核对 有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题。到期没有材料记\"未能判定\"，不算错。 不是重复 和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库 D211 ，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。 另外三条写法检查 评审 §1 第 1、5、6 条 。没过的问题不能照原样开，要重写；重写不了就不开，材料作证据。 原文支持： 主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天 D185、D210 。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。 口径完整： 算谁、不算谁、算什么写清楚；支出、融资、担保、订单、收入不混。 时间正确： 原文支持这个期间，不把几年的平均写成今年。 不开的 第 1 版 §6 的清单 不开 出处 它变成什么 季度数字和季度指引 第 1 版 §6；PRD 06 梯子第 6 条 证据 同一段指引里的每一行（\"费用率、税率是模型输入，不是争论\"） 第 1 版 §6 证据 没日期的\"护城河\"\"战略\" 第 1 版 §6 证据 高管任免、股东投票、回购授权；章程 第 1 版 §6；章程出自 D239 选定的 role-v29 原文（\"executive appointments, shareholder votes or bylaws\"） 证据或不留 例行诉讼程序 第 1 版 §6 证据 客户的部署计划（\"它是供应商需求和产品问题的证据，除非改变供应商自己的现金或信用风险\"） 第 1 版 §6 证据；改变公司自己的现金或信用风险时按 C 类判 以约束性义务为理由开：公司自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。它们不是约束性义务；对别的问题仍可以是证据，或按别的开题理由判 D236、D238 证据 把远期年度预测改装成事件 第 1 版 §6 证据 后年及以后的年度量（约束性义务除外） PRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D185 证据 同一个量的另一个估计、另一种口径、另一个来源 第 1 版 §6 测试 4 那个已开问题的证据，由匹配并入 同一题、续期，还是另一题：匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的 总规则： 同一个结果能判掉两题，就是同一题；答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注的，才是另一题 D248 。 同一题： 同一个结果能同时判两个问题。另一个估计、来源、措辞、方向 第 1 版 §6 测试 4 ；同一总量的另一种人群或代用口径（D190 碰撞裁决提示 collision-v1：\"another population or proxy of one aggregate\"；D197 引的 role-v12 原文：\"another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis\"）。候选并进已开问题，用它的结果档和到期日 PRD 06 。只有以共同量理由开题、带量的候选由代码自动判，其余是匹配和碰撞裁决的判断。 同一个计划的几种说法 （担保、融资承诺、信用支持……），最后核对的是同一个数，是一题 D248 第 1 条 。 同一件事的不同截止日： 早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上并进来的证据，不等新文章 D248 第 2 条 。 同一代产品的几个里程碑： 一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题 D248 第 3 条 。 公司自己的量： 只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据，两种年份差得多时分量低一点 D248 第 4 条；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条 。 身份： 事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并；对象、事件、截止日照旧核对 D248 第 5 条 。 续期： 同一个量或同一串事件的下一个期间 PRD 06 Succession、D190 。 另一题： 同一件事里答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的：有自己驱动的组成部分；同一笔交易或同一份附注里两个各自会变、分别盯着的数；另一件事 D190 碰撞裁决的四种；D248 第 1 条收窄了\"担保和融资\" 。 约束性义务： \"一笔或一个计划\"一题 D210 ；一笔交易是不是某个已按金额开题的计划的一部分，由匹配判，是就并入 D248 第 1 条 。还没签的单笔交易是\"签不签\"的是非题，照旧可以开，不并进计划的金额题 D210；PRD 06；D248 未改 。 共同量： 以共同量理由开题、带量的候选（量的类别是 other 的不算），由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判同一题；政策阶段和别的事件保留按事件判的身份；不带这个开题理由的主题量由模型判 D197 。 谁判，谁查 内容 谁 测试 1–3、同一篇文章里的测试 4、属于哪一类、问题怎么写 抽取的模型（判断） 结果档形状、期间是真日期、到期在文章之后、季度数、年限、约束性义务 18 个月、 settled_by 不为空 代码：只查形状，不判重要性 D154、D155、D184 和库里的同一题、续期、另一题 匹配（Jev）和碰撞裁决 D190 ；共同量由代码 D197 年限按公司财年数：2026 年 7 月时 NVIDIA 在 FY2027（2027 年 1 月结束），所以 FY2028 是明年。代码原来按自然年算，把 FY2028 当成后年拒掉；在 v29 答案的 40 个带年份的候选里拒错了 2 个。已修，测试是 tests/test_fiscal_year_horizon.py D243 。 5 证据标准 5.1 留的规则和四类 一个在追这家公司的分析员，读到会在本周笔记里写下来，因为它让某个问题的答案更可能或更不可能。 问题不限于库里已有的，也不限于本公司：客户、供应商、竞争者、监管、宏观都算 第 1 版 §2；D208 七类驱动 。 整体倾向谨慎，对增量敏感。 杂音很大，不是什么都要抽，不是什么都算线索。判一句话之前先问：这是新信息吗？它让哪个问题的答案变了？一件新发生的事、一个新数字、一个新的具体判断，是增量，不能漏；重复常识、描述长期状况、泛泛的看法，是杂音 D243 。增量和杂音的具体界线按 5.3 各行和边界一起判 D250 。 从第一段读到最后一段，分析员会记的（按本节和 5.3 各行边界一起判）都留。一页有八条就是八条，不挑 D199 。它们通常是下面四类之一；四类描述的是会记的陈述长什么样，不是\"属于四类就一定留\"，留不留由上面的规则判；具体不留的行和边界见 5.3。 公司自己的数字、指引或决定 第 1 版 §2 \"同店销售本季增长 4.2%\"；\"管理层把全年收入指引定在 120–130 亿美元\"；\"董事会批准 100 亿美元回购\"；\"公司取消软件订阅费，生产使用免费\" Alex 判 8，Omniverse 。 客户、供应商、竞争者、伙伴的有规模、有日期或有名字的动作，以及它们公布的数字 第 1 版 §2：三者有一个就够 第 1 版的例子\"Google Cloud 季度收入 248 亿美元，增长 82%，积压超过 5000 亿美元\" Alex 判 11 ；\"最大客户说明年订单砍五分之一\"；\"主要供应商说 2027 年产能翻倍\"；\"对手在欧洲降价 15%\"；\"一家银行选用竞争对手的系统\"；\"一家软件公司把本公司的模型集成进政府系统\"。后两条有名字、没金额，也留 D240 。 具名一方的主张或估计 第 1 版 §2 \"CFO 说十年前建的工厂仍满负荷\"；\"分析师把明年 EPS 预期上调到 4.10 美元\"；\"做空机构指控公司虚增应收 20 亿美元\"；\"内存厂说 2027 年短缺最严重，持续到 2030\"。 政策或监管的决定或步骤 第 1 版 §2 \"监管机构批准该药第二个适应症\"；\"政府要求该产品出口须申请许可\"；\"25 家公司联名反对过早限制开放权重模型\"；\"财长说将调查某国公司是否窃取知识产权\"。 典型的主张和背景 D243 第 5 条 ：Alex 的原话是\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"。常识，就是跟这家公司的分析员读这篇之前就已经知道的：谁领先、谁在竞争、需求强、供给紧、融资难这类说法，和对已知状况的重述。这类陈述没有数字和具体的叙事就不收。不是常识的，是新信息，比如一份新报告的判断、一个新的市场数据、刚发生的事的细节，照上面的规则判，分析员会记就留。 常识，不收： \"博通 CEO 说能从英伟达抢份额\"；\"Blackwell 和 Rubin 仍然供不应求\"；\"融资会是扩大算力最大的障碍\"；\"内存厂避开了以往的产能过剩\"；\"博通预计明年随着新客户推出定制芯片大幅增长\"。 新信息，收： 一位科技公司高管对媒体说\"中国国产 AI 芯片厂商已经全部卖光，客户在考虑性能更低的芯片\"；\"旧型号 GPU 的租金在涨\"；研究机构说\"这让 Rubin Ultra 暂时没有成熟的扩展方案，给对手留出了空间\"；\"华为现在占中国 AI 芯片市场约一半，这个市场约 500 亿美元\"。 有数字也要过上面的规则： 作者说\"英特尔多年领先，占 CPU 市场 59%\"，和英伟达的问题关系不大，分析员不会记。 5.2 不完整也是证据 预测、传闻、反证、远期、条件情景都是证据；它们的层级和确定性另记。不因为\"还没发生\"\"不是官方\"\"离现在远\"\"和我们方向相反\"就不留；一个真实的反例可能比十个顺风数字更重要 第 1 版 §2 。有名有姓的动作缺金额、缺日期也是证据，分量可以低一点 D240 。不留清单只按 5.3 各行和边界删，不能用来删掉标准要留的陈述：\"a reject rule never removes what the standard keeps\" D240 。 5.3 不留的 第 1 版 §2 的清单，加 D238、D243 的行 \"按同一行补的例子\"一列是我按同一行举的例子。 不留 第 1 版的例子 按同一行补的例子 边界 没有业务规模的产品、游戏、驱动、跑分细节 \"这张卡 415W\"；\"Geekbench 跑分\" 接口、功率、旧型号的支持周期 对手拿它和本公司产品比的说法不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判（Alex 判 4：AMD 2.4 倍是辅助）；伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不是产品细节（5.1 第 2 类）；公司自己、伙伴或第三方发布的对比（\"比闭源模型便宜 10 倍\"、\"同样功率多放 40% GPU\"、第三方测试）是产品细节，不收；同一句里伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不因这一条删，照旧按 5.1 判 D250 第 3 条 股价涨跌、投资人仓\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/main-numbers/reader-packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+a[\\\"answer\\\"]+\\\" art\\\"+str(a[\\\"article\\\"])),[print(\\\"Q\\\",q[\\\"id\\\"],q[\\\"question\\\"],\\\"/\\\",q.get(\\\"outcome\\\",\\\"\\\"),\\\"/\\\",q.get(\\\"period\\\",\\\"\\\")[:140]) for q in a[\\\"new_questions\\\"]],[print(\\\"S\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"LINK\\\",[l[\\\"question\\\"] for l in s.get(\\\"linked_to\\\",[]) if l.get(\\\"new\\\")]) for s in a.get(\\\"kept_statements\\\",[]) if any(x in s[\\\"summary\\\"].lower() for x in [\\\"406.05\\\",\\\"671.95\\\",\\\"412 billion\\\",\\\"70%\\\",\\\"72% to 73%\\\",\\\"72%–73%\\\",\\\"72% and 73%\\\"])]) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"] if int(a[\\\"answer\\\"][1:])<=36]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/main-numbers/reader-packet.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+a[\\\"answer\\\"]+\\\" art\\\"+str(a[\\\"article\\\"])),[print(\\\"Q\\\",q[\\\"id\\\"],q[\\\"question\\\"],\\\"/\\\",q.get(\\\"outcome\\\",\\\"\\\"),\\\"/\\\",q.get(\\\"period\\\",\\\"\\\")[:140]) for q in a[\\\"new_questions\\\"]],[print(\\\"S\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"LINK\\\",[l[\\\"question\\\"] for l in s.get(\\\"linked_to\\\",[]) if l.get(\\\"new\\\")]) for s in a.get(\\\"kept_statements\\\",[]) if any(x in s[\\\"summary\\\"].lower() for x in [\\\"406.05\\\",\\\"671.95\\\",\\\"412 billion\\\",\\\"70%\\\",\\\"72% to 73%\\\",\\\"72%–73%\\\",\\\"72% and 73%\\\"])]) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"] if int(a[\\\"answer\\\"][1:])<=36]'\"","aggregated_output":"\nA01 art4\nQ new-supply-commitments What total of supply commitments and purchase obligations will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report? / The disclosed amount of supply commitments/purchase obligations NVIDIA has taken on, as reported in its next annual report, and how it compares with prior disclosures. / Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report fo\n\nA02 art10\nS e3 On NVIDIA's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \"a supply-constrained outlook\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand. LINK []\nS e4 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said on the company's late-August earnings call that gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect, down from 75% in the fiscal second quarter. LINK []\nS e5 The article states that analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, and applying the guided roughly 70% growth to that figure derives fiscal 2028 revenue of somewhere around $700 billion. LINK []\n\nA03 art2\nS ev5 Zacks reported that consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA are $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year, indicating changes of +88% and +65.5%, respectively. LINK []\n\nA04 art10\nQ new-fy2028-revenue Will NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028) revenue grow roughly 70% year over year from fiscal 2027, as CFO Colette Kress guided on the late-August 2026 earnings call, or will the supply-constrained outlook produce a different total? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 total revenue and its growth rate versus fiscal 2027, compared with the roughly 70% growth the CFO guided (implying roughly $700 billion if the ~$412 billion fiscal 2027 consensus holds). / Fiscal year 2028 (ending January 2028); settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February 2028 \nQ new-fy2028-gross-margin Where will NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 gross margin settle, versus the 72%–73% CFO Colette Kress guided and the 75% reported in fiscal 2027's second quarter? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin and the fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter trough, compared with the guided 71%–72% bottom in fiscal Q4 2027 and 72%–73% in fiscal 2028. / Fiscal year 2028 (ending January 2028), with fiscal 2027's fourth quarter as the guided trough; settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 results and \nQ new-fy2027-revenue Will NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027) revenue come in around the analysts' consensus of about $412 billion, roughly 90% growth, given fiscal Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and fiscal Q3 guidance of $108 billion? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2027 total revenue versus the ~$412 billion consensus and the ~90% year-over-year growth expectation. / Fiscal year 2027 (ending late January 2027); settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February \nS ev-3 On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and characterised the outlook as \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue']\nS ev-4 On the same late-August 2026 call, CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA's gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal 2027 fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in, a drop from the 75% gross margin of the fiscal 2027 second quarter. LINK ['new-fy2028-gross-margin']\nS ev-5 Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in at around $412 billion. LINK ['new-fy2027-revenue']\n\nA05 art2\nQ revenue-fy2027 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027, its current fiscal year? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027, compared with the Zacks consensus estimate of $406.05 billion cited in the 22 September 2026 item. / Fiscal 2027 (current fiscal year ending late January 2027) — settled by NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 Form 10-K and fiscal-year earnings release, exp\nQ revenue-fy2028 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, its next fiscal year? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028, compared with the Zacks consensus estimate of $671.95 billion cited in the 22 September 2026 item. / Fiscal 2028 (next fiscal year ending late January 2028) — settled by NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 Form 10-K and fiscal-year earnings release, expect\nS e-sales-current-next-fy Zacks reported consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA of $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year, indicating changes of +88% and +65.5%, respectively. LINK ['revenue-fy2027', 'revenue-fy2028']\n\nA06 art10\nQ new-1 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027 (the year ending January 2027)? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027, versus the roughly $412 billion analysts' consensus cited in the item and the trajectory implied by fiscal Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and fiscal Q3 guidance of $108 billion. / Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected a\nQ new-2 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), given management's guidance of roughly 70% growth on a supply-constrained outlook? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028 versus the roughly 70% growth management guided, and whether the constraint proves to be manufacturing capacity rather than demand. / Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected a\nQ new-3 What gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), after management guided it to bottom at 71%–72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and settle at 72%–73% in fiscal 2028? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin versus the 72%–73% management guidance and the fiscal Q2 2027 level of 75%, and whether the contemplated price increases materialise. / Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled at the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected a\nS ev-3 On NVIDIA's late-August fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and called it \"a supply-constrained outlook\", with the limit being manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand. LINK ['new-2']\nS ev-4 On the same earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in, a small drop from the 75% gross margin reported in the fiscal second quarter. LINK ['new-3']\nS ev-5 Analysts' consensus had NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. LINK ['new-1']\n\nA07 art9\nQ N1 Will NVIDIA report fiscal 2028 total revenue growth of about 70% year over year, as management has guided in its first full-year outlook? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 total revenue and year-over-year growth versus the ~70% management guidance. / Fiscal 2028 (fiscal year ending late January 2028); settled by NVIDIA's Q4 FY2028 results and FY2028 Form 10-K, expected around February 202\nQ N2 What gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, against its guidance of a 72%–73% range as memory prices rise? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin versus the 72%–73% guided range, given higher-than-expected memory prices and price increases already executed. / Fiscal 2028 (fiscal year ending late January 2028); settled by NVIDIA's Q4 FY2028 results and FY2028 Form 10-K, expected around February 202\nS e3 NVIDIA management guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70% year over year — the first time it has guided a full year ahead — and the CEO said demand is much greater than 70%, with supply, not demand, holding the guide at that level. LINK ['N1']\nS e5 NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower, to a 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices rose more than management expected, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. LINK ['N2']\n\nA08 art9\nQ new-1 How fast will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in fiscal 2028 (the year ending late January 2028), against management's guidance of about 70% growth? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 total revenue and its year-over-year growth rate, compared with the roughly 70% growth management guided in September 2026. / Fiscal year 2028 (ending late January 2028), settled at the release of NVIDIA's FY2028 results and Form 10-K around February 2028 — set per \nQ new-2 Will NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 gross margin land in the 72%–73% range management guided, after memory prices rose more than expected? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin versus the 72%–73% range guided in September 2026. / Fiscal year 2028 (ending late January 2028), settled at the release of NVIDIA's FY2028 results and Form 10-K around February 2028 — a [reaso\nS e3 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. Its CEO said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide at that level. LINK ['new-1']\nS e5 NVIDIA guided its gross margin to settle in the 72%–73% range in fiscal 2028, after memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. LINK ['new-2']\n\nA09 art6\nQ new-nvda-fy27-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027? / NVIDIA's consolidated revenue for fiscal year 2027. / Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027, expected around February 2027, will report the annual figur\nQ new-nvda-fy27-gross-margin What non-GAAP gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027? / NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin for fiscal year 2027. / Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027, expected around February 2027, will report the annual gross\n\nA10 art8\nS e5 NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, while cautioning that supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK []\nS e6 The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenues stands at $406.05 billion, which the article says implies an 88% year-over-year increase. LINK []\n\nA11 art10\nQ new-nvda-fy2027-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027, the year ending late January 2027? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027, compared with the fiscal Q2 actual of $96.2 billion, fiscal Q3 guidance of $108 billion and analysts' consensus of about $412 billion. / NVIDIA fiscal 2027 annual results (Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the release of that fiscal year's results, per [reason].\nQ new-nvda-fy2028-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028, compared with the CFO's expectation of roughly 70% growth and the article's calculation of about $700 billion. / NVIDIA fiscal 2028 annual results (Form 10-K expected around February 2028) — the release of that fiscal year's results, per [reason].\nQ new-nvda-fy2028-gross-margin What gross margin will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028? / NVIDIA's reported fiscal 2028 gross margin, compared with the CFO's expectation that it settles at 72% to 73%, after bottoming at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and versus 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. / NVIDIA fiscal 2028 annual results (Form 10-K expected around February 2028) — the release of that fiscal year's results, per [reason].\nS e3 On the late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and called it \"a supply-constrained outlook\"; the article paraphrased this as manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, being the limit. LINK ['new-nvda-fy2028-revenue']\nS e4 In this article, analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, and the article elsewhere refers to \"this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth\"; applying the CFO's roughly 70% growth rate implies fiscal 2028 revenue of somewhere around $700 billion. LINK ['new-nvda-fy2027-revenue', 'new-nvda-fy2028-revenue']\nS e5 NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said on the call that gross margins should bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027 and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in; the article notes this compares with fiscal Q2 2027 gross margin of 75%. LINK ['new-nvda-fy2028-gross-margin']\n\nA12 art5\nS e3 Nvidia's revenue more than doubled year over year in its most recent quarter, and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, according to qz.com. The 70% figure is company guidance; the 45% figure was the prior consensus. LINK []\n\nA13 art2\nS ev-5 Consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA are $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year, indicating changes of +88% and +65.5% respectively. LINK []\n\nA14 art6\n\nA15 art5\nQ nt-1 How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in fiscal 2027, against the roughly 90%-or-more growth consensus reported in this item? / NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 total revenue growth versus fiscal 2026, against the roughly 90%-or-more consensus expectation. / Fiscal 2027 results release (Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the current fiscal year's annual results.\nQ nt-2 Will NVIDIA's total revenue grow about 70% in fiscal 2028, as the company projected? / NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 total revenue growth versus fiscal 2027, against the company's 70% projection. / Fiscal 2028 results release (Form 10-K expected around February 2028) — the next fiscal year's annual results.\nQ nt-3 What will NVIDIA's gross margin be for fiscal 2027, against the 75% reported last quarter and analyst estimates of sub-72% by the fourth quarter? / NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 annual gross margin and its quarterly trajectory, against the 75% reported last quarter and the sub-72% fourth-quarter estimate. / Fiscal 2027 annual report (Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the current fiscal year's annual gross margin and fourth-quarter resul\nS ev-1 NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, according to Quartz's report on the company's second-quarter earnings. LINK ['nt-2']\n\nA16 art1\n\nA17 art3\n\nA18 art2\nQ rev_fy27 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for its current fiscal year (the fiscal year ending late January 2027)? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for the current fiscal year, to be compared with the $406.05 billion consensus sales estimate cited in the 22 September 2026 Zacks article. / Current fiscal year (FY2027, ending late January 2027) — set from the item's 'current fiscal year' consensus estimate; settled by NVIDIA's r\nQ rev_fy28 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for its next fiscal year (the fiscal year ending late January 2028)? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for the next fiscal year, to be compared with the $671.95 billion consensus sales estimate cited in the 22 September 2026 Zacks article. / Next fiscal year (FY2028, ending late January 2028) — set from the item's 'next fiscal year' consensus estimate; settled by the release of t\nS e4 Zacks reported consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA of $109.02 billion for the current quarter (+91.2% year over year) and, for the current and next fiscal years, $406.05 billion (+88%) and $671.95 billion (+65.5%), respectively, as of its 22 September 2026 article. LINK ['rev_fy27', 'rev_fy28']\n\nA19 art3\n\nA20 art9\nS e3 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth to about 70%, the first time it has guided a full year ahead; CEO Jensen Huang said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there. LINK []\nS e5 NVIDIA guided fiscal 2028 gross margin to settle in the 72% to 73% range, lower, as memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed. LINK []\n\nA21 art4\n\nA22 art5\nQ new-1 What revenue growth will Nvidia report for fiscal 2028, and what revenue level does it imply, against the 70% growth the company guided to at its fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings? / Nvidia's reported fiscal 2028 revenue growth (and resulting revenue), versus the 70% growth management projected in September 2026 and the ~45% growth analysts had expected before that guidance. / Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled by the fiscal 2028 Form 10-K expected around February 2028, per section 3 ([reason] qua\nQ new-2 What gross margin will Nvidia report for fiscal 2027, after 75% in the second quarter and analyst estimates of under 72% by the fourth quarter on rising memory-component costs? / Nvidia's reported fiscal 2027 gross margin versus the 75% second-quarter level and the under-72% fourth-quarter analyst estimate. / Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by the fiscal 2027 Form 10-K expected around February 2027, per section 3 ([reason] qua\nS ev-4 Nvidia guided to 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 when it reported second-quarter earnings, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time. LINK ['new-1']\n\nA23 art10\nS e3 On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), calling it \"a supply-constrained outlook\" — i.e., the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand, as reported by fool.com. LINK []\nS e4 On the same late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in, a drop from the 75% gross margin reported for the fiscal 2027 second quarter, as reported by fool.com. LINK []\nS e5 Analysts' consensus, as cited by fool.com, had NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion. LINK []\n\nA24 art6\nQ new-1 Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin data-center platform — the successor generation of the product line that carries the company's data-center value — reach first customer shipments by the end of fiscal 2027? / First revenue shipments of the Vera Rubin platform, after the company reported it entered full production, confirmed by NVIDIA's own shipment/revenue commentary in quarterly results and SEC filings. / NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date because the item reports full production bu\n\nA25 art7\n\nA26 art8\nQ new-1 What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027 (the year ending late January 2027)? / NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 total revenue, versus the Zacks consensus of $406.05 billion (+88% year over year), given second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion (+106% year over year, Data Center $89.02 billion, +117%) and third-quarter guidance of $108 billion +/- 2%. / Fiscal year 2027 (ending late January 2027); settled at NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter results and Form 10-K, expected around February \nS e3 NVIDIA said it expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, while cautioning that supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK ['new-1']\nS e14 The Zacks Consensus Estimate put NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at $406.05 billion, an 88% year-over-year increase. LINK ['new-1']\n\nA27 art7\n\nA28 art9\nS ev-3 NVIDIA management guided about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, the first time it has given a full-year-ahead guide; the CEO said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide at that level, according to Trefis. LINK []\nS ev-5 NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower, to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed, according to Trefis. LINK []\n\nA29 art10\nS ev3 On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), calling it \"a supply-constrained outlook\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand. LINK []\nS ev4 On the earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect, down from 75% in the fiscal second quarter. LINK []\nS ev5 Analysts' consensus had NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue at around $412 billion, according to the article. LINK []\n\nA30 art8\nQ new-fy2027-total-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027? / NVIDIA's total revenue for fiscal 2027, compared with the $96.22 billion reported in Q2 fiscal 2027, the $108 billion Q3 fiscal 2027 guidance and the $406.05 billion Zacks Consensus Estimate. / Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), settled by NVIDIA's FY2027 Form 10-K expected around February 2027.\nQ new-fy2028-revenue-growth What year-over-year revenue growth will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2028? / NVIDIA's total revenue growth for fiscal 2028, compared with the company's expectation of about 70% growth. / Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028), settled by NVIDIA's FY2028 Form 10-K expected around February 2028.\nS e5_fy2028_revenue_growth_guidance NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK ['new-fy2028-revenue-growth']\nS e6_zacks_fy2027_revenue The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue is $406.05 billion, implying an 88% year-over-year increase. LINK ['new-fy2027-total-revenue']\n\nA31 art8\nQ nvda-fy2027-total-revenue How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for fiscal 2027, the year that carries the business? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal year 2027 (year ending late January 2027), judged against the Zacks consensus of $406.05 billion and the run-rate implied by $96.22 billion of second-quarter revenue and $108 billion third-quarter guidance. / Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by NVIDIA's fourth-quarter and full-year fiscal 2027 results and Form 10-K, expected ar\nS e5-fy2028-revenue-growth-outlook NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the article reports, adding that supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand. LINK []\nS e6-zacks-fy2027-revenue-consensus The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue is $406.05 billion, implying an 88% year-over-year increase, Zacks reported. LINK ['nvda-fy2027-total-revenue']\n\nA32 art4\n\nA33 art3\n\nA34 art6\nQ new-1 Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform — the next-generation data-center GPU system that carries the company's value — reach first customer shipments (revenue-generating deliveries) by the end of fiscal 2027? / Whether NVIDIA confirms first customer shipments, and revenue, from Vera Rubin systems; the item reports the platform entered full production in the period covered by the Aug. 26, 2026 press release (fiscal Q2 2027). / NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date, since the item reports full-production ent\n\nA35 art2\nQ new-fy27-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for its current fiscal year (fiscal 2027, ending late January 2027), versus the Zacks consensus of $406.05 billion (+88% year over year) as of 22 September 2026? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2027 and its growth versus fiscal 2026, compared with the consensus estimate of $406.05 billion (+88%). / Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); settled by the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 fourth-quarter and full-year results, expected a\nQ new-fy28-revenue What total revenue will NVIDIA report for its next fiscal year (fiscal 2028, ending late January 2028), versus the Zacks consensus of $671.95 billion (+65.5%) as of 22 September 2026? / NVIDIA's reported total revenue for fiscal 2028 and its growth versus fiscal 2027, compared with the consensus estimate of $671.95 billion (+65.5%). / Fiscal 2028 (year ending late January 2028); settled by the release of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 fourth-quarter and full-year results, expected a\nS ev-annual-sales Zacks' consensus sales estimates for NVIDIA are $406.05 billion for the current fiscal year (fiscal 2027) and $671.95 billion for the next fiscal year (fiscal 2028), indicating changes of +88% and +65.5% respectively. LINK ['new-fy27-revenue', 'new-fy28-revenue']\n\nA36 art9\nS ev3 NVIDIA management guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the first time it has given a full-year guide; CEO says demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there, while the CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. LINK []\nS ev4 NVIDIA guided its gross margin to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lower as memory costs climb; memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed, according to Trefis. LINK []\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
